const WRITEUPS2027 = {"p301850c":{"text":"The 2025 AL Cy Young runner-up and Boston's Opening Day starter, and the club's medical staff is now under internal review over how his 2026 was handled. What was supposed to be a start or two on the IL in April became five months; he came back in the Wild Card Series touching 96.5 in live BPs. Breslow says he's on track for a fully healthy, normal offseason with no restrictions. The durability questions are real, and so is the upside: an ace-level arm the room may discount.","sources":[{"note_id":"n5f92b74216","article_date":"2026-08-23","source":"MLB.com","author":"Theo DeRosa","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/garrett-crochet-contract-opt-out-voided-due-to-injury-stint"},{"note_id":"n2ca4531346","article_date":"2026-09-29","source":"MLB.com","author":"Ian Browne","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/garrett-crochet-al-wild-card-series-roster"},{"note_id":"n7c528d4f35","article_date":"2026-11-13","source":"MLB.com","author":"Ian Browne","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/red-sox-to-review-medical-practices-after-injury-plagued-2026"},{"note_id":"ne5a812e1b2","article_date":"2026-10-05","source":"CBS Sports","author":"RotoWire Staff","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/red-soxs-garrett-crochet-expected-to-have-normal-offseason/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Garrett Crochet","team":"BOS","elig":"SP"},"p5c9bcf3":{"text":"Third in the 2026 AL MVP vote in what was, by his standards, a down year. The 18 homers and 54 RBI were career lows, but the walk rate climbed to 10.6% and his exit velocity, hard-hit rate and line-drive rate all improved. Razzball blames the power dip on a June MCL sprain and a July back strain, his first career IL stint, and expects him fully healthy for 2027. He led the AL with 30 steals through 94 games, and Kansas City lists him first among the core that's here to stay.","sources":[{"note_id":"n3752138c8b","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n8f91a0e2a5","article_date":"2026-08-05","source":"MLB.com","author":"Anne Rogers","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/bobby-witt-jr-activated-by-royals"},{"note_id":"n9ca27c9999","article_date":"2026-09-27","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jackson Stone","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/royals-2027-outlook-after-disappointing-2026-season"},{"note_id":"n438e082fe2","article_date":"2026-11-13","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian McTaggart","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/yordan-alvarez-wins-2026-al-mvp-award"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Bobby Witt Jr.","team":"KCR","elig":"SS"},"pabd0d7f":{"text":"Cleveland signed him for $87,500 and traded him to Tampa for a Rule 5-eligible Triple-A arm; Guardians president Chris Antonetti later called it \"a bad trade.\" The Rays said the ball sounded different off his bat. Since then: 45 homers in 2025, then 42 homers, 101 RBI and a .276/.360/.524 line over all 162 games in 2026, finishing second in the AL MVP vote. Razzball calls him a future MVP and the No. 1 dynasty third baseman. He played through lower-body soreness in the World Series but started Game 5.","sources":[{"note_id":"nb524bcb73d","article_date":"2026-10-11","source":"MLB.com","author":"Adam Berry","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/junior-caminero-facing-team-that-traded-him-to-rays-in-2026-alcs"},{"note_id":"n9f6b16d221","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-third-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n283864ea08","article_date":"2026-11-13","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian McTaggart","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/yordan-alvarez-wins-2026-al-mvp-award"},{"note_id":"naa441e08bd","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"MLB.com","author":"Adam Berry","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/junior-caminero-injury-status-world-series-game-5"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Junior Caminero","team":"TBR","elig":"3B"},"p87322e2":{"text":"The breakout came one year late: after a .157 rookie season, he led the 2026 Royals in average (.289), slugging and OPS with 23 homers and took over the No. 3 spot in the lineup. Most of his injuries came from playing the outfield, including a wall crash, a diving catch and a flexor strain on a 95-mph throw. That's where the helium is: MLB.com notes a Pasquantino trade could hand him first base for good in 2027.","sources":[{"note_id":"na4020e78b3","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-first-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nb5926c29ae","article_date":"2026-10-21","source":"MLB.com","author":"Perla Paredes","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jac-caglianone-s-versatility-could-be-key-for-royals-in-2027"},{"note_id":"n855dd1b4af","article_date":"2026-09-27","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jackson Stone","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/royals-2027-outlook-after-disappointing-2026-season"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Jac Caglianone","team":"KCR","elig":"1B/OF"},"p8c74fc1":{"text":"Even in a broken season he slugged .511 with 18 homers in 66 games. A fractured rib cost him 84 games, and a moderate calf strain kept him off the postseason roster. The Yankees expect him ready for Spring Training. He turns 35 in April, and SI suggests more DH days are coming, which protects his bat.","sources":[{"note_id":"nc1e89e2107","article_date":"2026-10-03","source":"MLB.com","author":"Bryan Hoch","url":"https://www.mlb.com/yankees/news/yankees-aaron-judge-injury-update-ahead-of-alds"},{"note_id":"n044b3482f9","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/yankees-injuries-and-roster-moves"},{"note_id":"ndbea3360e3","article_date":"2026-10-20","source":"Sports Illustrated","author":"Michael Zeno","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/yankees/onsi/projecting-the-yankees-2027-opening-day-lineup-new-york-makes-changes-at-middle-infield-spots-01m55c7ggjnp"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Aaron Judge","team":"NYY","elig":"OF"},"p7f1b103":{"text":"A seven-time All-Star, six-time Silver Slugger and Cleveland's icon, and he still stole 37 bases in the worst offensive season of his career. Blame the hamate: he broke it June 13, rushed back a week early, and hit two homers in his final 53 games with pain he said would last all year. MLB.com wrote he might not have been 50 percent of himself. Hamate power typically returns with time, and Razzball doesn't see the start of a decline at 34. A healthy wrist means a buy-low on a speed-power third baseman.","sources":[{"note_id":"nfcd9ed4ec3","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-third-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n14dcc4ad52","article_date":"2026-10-07","source":"MLB.com","author":"Martín Gallegos","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jose-ramirez-struggling-for-guardians-2026-alds"},{"note_id":"n4086f7863c","article_date":"2026-09-19","source":"MLB.com","author":"Tim Stebbins","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jose-ramirez-homers-for-1-000-career-rbis"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Jose Ramirez","team":"CLE","elig":"3B"},"pf97704":{"text":"The 2025 AL Rookie of the Year hit 36 homers with a .619 slugging, then had 21 homers and 69 RBI through 99 games in 2026 before a right thumb injury ended his season July 31. He was taking batting practice in the final week, and the A's expect him ready for Spring Training. If the thumb is right, he's a first-round bat.","sources":[{"note_id":"nce1f7f03be","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-first-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n5aa0908fd3","article_date":"2026-08-21","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jeremy Rakes, Jackson Stone","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jacob-wilson-nick-kurtz-expected-to-miss-rest-of-2026-season"},{"note_id":"n92e7db0d47","article_date":"2026-09-16","source":"CBS Sports","author":"RotoWire Staff","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/athletics-nick-kurtz-begins-hitting-progression/"},{"note_id":"nfb2aed2c7b","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/athletics-injuries-and-roster-moves"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Nick Kurtz","team":"ATH","elig":"1B"},"p9ccea09":{"text":"A three-time All-Star coming off his weakest season at age 25: 20 homers through 127 games and a .706 OPS, a year after a 32-homer, 30-steal campaign. The excuses are there: a concussion that cost him most of July, a team-wide slump and an overhauled coaching staff. His bat speed is still in the 95th percentile and his expected average barely moved. The fix is the 39% chase rate and poor squared-up contact. SI calls writing him off \"reckless.\"","sources":[{"note_id":"nd1100565c0","article_date":"2026-11-15","source":"MLB.com","author":"Mike Petriello","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/how-the-mariners-can-fix-defensive-issues-in-2027"},{"note_id":"n8a11c96b7a","article_date":"2026-09-01","source":"Sports Illustrated","author":"Tremayne Person","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/mariners/onsi/mariners-must-fix-the-real-problem-behind-julio-rodriguez-s-2026-regression"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Julio Rodriguez","team":"SEA","elig":"OF"},"p28aaca5":{"text":"A seventh-round pick in 2022 whom scouts never saw coming, and now the unanimous AL Cy Young winner in his first full season. He went 14-6 with a 1.95 ERA, the AL's lowest since Verlander and the Yankees' lowest since Guidry in 1978, with 239 strikeouts and a majors-best 21 starts allowing one run or none. He got better as the year went on, with a 1.17 ERA over his final nine starts while holding his velocity. Boone calls him a foundational player.","sources":[{"note_id":"n7d2cd13c67","article_date":"2026-10-15","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-100-dynasty-starting-pitchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n8c38fcb9f9","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"MLB.com","author":"Bryan Hoch","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/cam-schlittler-wins-2026-al-cy-young-award"},{"note_id":"n51e2574881","article_date":"2026-09-25","source":"MLB.com","author":"Bryan Hoch","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/cam-schlittler-finishes-2026-regular-season-with-a-1-95-era"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Cam Schlittler","team":"NYY","elig":"SP"},"pbd68516":{"text":"Three straight seasons of 20-20: 23, 26 and 27 homers with 30, 26 and 24 steals, and the Angels' bWAR leader each year. He played a career-high 157 games after 2025 shoulder surgery. The walk rate jumped to 9.9% and pushed his OBP to a career-high .330. After a first half he said didn't feel right, he turned it around following the All-Star break, and his stated 2027 goal is to carry that over. Under control through 2029, he's either the cornerstone for new ownership or a trade chip, so his team could change before draft day.","sources":[{"note_id":"n3fa15640f6","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"ne80e24edb3","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"MLB.com","author":"Rhett Bollinger","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/zach-neto-gives-angels-consistency-at-shortstop"}],"as_of":"2026-11-19","approved_by":"Eno","approved_date":"2026-11-19","name":"Zach Neto","team":"LAA","elig":"SS"},"pf29d0da":{"text":"Last July he sent a 113-mph, 420-foot homer off Caleb Thielbar into the left-field second deck at Camden Yards, only the eighth ball ever to land there, and Pete Alonso said it \"would have gone out of Yosemite.\" His high school coach called it \"light-tower power,\" and in 2020 Baltimore talked him out of Florida, his \"dream school,\" with a roughly $1.75 million bonus well over slot, paid for with the savings from signing No. 2 pick Heston Kjerstad under slot, the Mike Elias \"portfolio approach.\" His 2026 split in two: .193/.264/.395 in the first half and .270/.337/.540 in the second, helped by a May throwing program that cleaned up his arm at third and a move closer to the plate. The worry is where he plays, with Westburg, Alexander and Encarnacion-Strand also at third and Elias floating outfield reps; SI's Jason La Canfora wants the Orioles to commit to 500 plate appearances, while Bleacher Report's Joel Reuter has him at DH and says he won't be pushed to the bench. He posted a 1.105 OPS against lefties, best in the game per the Banner, is working on righties this winter, and at not yet 25 with 24 homers he's a bat Baltimore has every reason to find at-bats for.","sources":[{"note_id":"nce0d32b624","article_date":"2026-07-09","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jake Rill","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/coby-mayo-hits-second-deck-home-run-in-orioles-loss"},{"note_id":"ncfe0866072","article_date":"2023-05-24","source":"PressBox","author":"Luke Jackson","url":"https://pressboxonline.com/2023/05/24/pipeline-primer-orioles-prospect-coby-mayo/"},{"note_id":"n2e082aa19f","article_date":"2020-06-16","source":"MLB.com","author":"Joe Trezza","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/orioles-agree-with-fourth-round-pick-coby-mayo"},{"note_id":"n1eefc5b8ac","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Coby_Mayo"},{"note_id":"n6821615d6e","article_date":"2020-07-01","source":"MLB.com","author":"Joe Trezza","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/heston-kjerstad-signs-with-orioles"},{"note_id":"n79394e1ead","article_date":"2022-06-28","source":"AZ Snake Pit (SB Nation)","author":"Michael McDermott","url":"https://www.azsnakepit.com/2022/6/28/23157603/examining-the-houston-astros-baltimore-orioles-general-manager-first-round-draft-history"},{"note_id":"n46c4b6c5f7","article_date":"","source":"Baseball-Reference","author":"","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/players/split.fcgi?id=mayoco01&year=2026&t=b"},{"note_id":"n7f6df1d53b","article_date":"2026-10-30","source":"The Baltimore Banner","author":"Jon Meoli","url":"https://www.thebanner.com/sports/orioles-mlb/orioles-coby-mayo-VNYNABHSWBFORMSMNO5HDBGV3Q/"},{"note_id":"n09fb8d5248","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"The Baltimore Banner","author":"Andy Kostka","url":"https://www.thebanner.com/sports/orioles-mlb/orioles-infielders-moving-to-outfield-coby-mayo-jordan-westburg-AQX7FK3O3VAPNO6K3NEYLCKHNE/"},{"note_id":"n9efeca3035","article_date":"2026-10-12","source":"Sports Illustrated (On SI)","author":"Jason La Canfora","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/orioles/onsi/the-orioles-must-commit-to-coby-mayo-for-2027-01m4s3c7tzb1"},{"note_id":"nb8abfd616f","article_date":"2026-09-10","source":"Bleacher Report","author":"Joel Reuter","url":"https://bleacherreport.com/articles/25497410-predicting-every-mlb-teams-2027-opening-day-lineup"},{"note_id":"n738144a764","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-first-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Coby Mayo","team":"BAL","elig":"1B/3B/CI"},"p895e8b6":{"text":"Toronto gave him a $3.9 million bonus on July 2, 2015, and a 14-year, $500 million extension in April 2025, and he answered the extension by winning the 2025 ALCS MVP. Then came the worst season of his career: .260/.332/.356 with nine homers in 609 plate appearances after a .354 March and April, plus his first career IL stint, a concussion from an Aug. 14 collision at third base. The contact went with it, his barrel rate falling from 12.2% to 6.9% and his hard-hit rate from 50.7% to 44.3%, and Joey Votto pinned it on a forward move that delays the barrel from snapping into place. He had laser eye surgery after the season, and the Blue Jays' Atkins says he's \"very confident\" Vlad will bounce back.","sources":[{"note_id":"n65f03b7e3b","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Vladimir_Guerrero_Jr."},{"note_id":"nadea76ccc2","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"MLB.com","author":"Manny Randhawa","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/vladimir-guerrero-jr-keys-to-bounceback-season-in-2027"},{"note_id":"nb1cb6f063d","article_date":"2026-09-30","source":"theScore","author":"Tom Ruminski","url":"https://www.thescore.com/mlb/news/3612434/blue-jays-atkins-very-confident-guerrero-will-bounce-back-in-2027"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Vladimir Guerrero Jr.","team":"TOR","elig":"1B/CI"},"p95e60af":{"text":"On June 11, 2023, he hit a 462-foot homer onto Eutaw Street, the longest ever to land there in a regulation game, on his way to a unanimous AL Rookie of the Year award. Baltimore took him in the second round in 2019, the same draft it took Adley Rutschman first overall, and he followed up as Baseball America's 2022 Minor League Player of the Year and a 37-homer, fourth-in-the-MVP-vote star in 2024. The last two years haven't matched that: a mild intercostal strain to open 2025, then a career-low .225 with a .712 OPS in 2026, though he still hit 24 homers and played 158 games. Mike Elias says he's been \"a little bit banged up\" while playing every day and fully expects a much better season: \"He'll be back in the saddle.\" The offseason trade talk is only talk, as Elias calls his core players \"very unlikely\" to go anywhere, and Razzball's Jakkers believes the 25-year-old can and will get back to his 2023-24 level.","sources":[{"note_id":"nbed4107228","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Gunnar_Henderson"},{"note_id":"n83a790b799","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"BaltimoreBaseball.com","author":"Rich Dubroff","url":"https://www.baltimorebaseball.com/sports/orioles-mlb/2026/11/10/orioles-dont-seem-eager-trade-henderson-hell-back-saddle-richdubroff/"},{"note_id":"n188d0426f9","article_date":"2026-11-11","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jake Rill","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mike-elias-orioles-trade-rumors-2026-27-offseason"},{"note_id":"nb3dec7b6f9","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Gunnar Henderson","team":"BAL","elig":"SS/MI"},"p953f48f":{"text":"His first Orioles season was a monster: an AL-leading 43 homers and 113 RBI, making him the first player ever to lead both leagues in RBIs, plus his second straight Silver Slugger and first-team All-MLB honors. He hit career homer No. 300 in his return to Citi Field on Sept. 15 and played all 161 games, stretching his streak to 577 straight, 23rd-most in history. A 2016 second-rounder out of Florida, he opted out of his Mets deal and signed with Baltimore for five years and $155 million. SI's Jason La Canfora expects regression at 32 in a swing-and-miss lineup and doubts 43 and 113 happen again. He hasn't missed a game to injury in five years, and this winter's trade talk in Baltimore has asked whether he's the one untouchable piece on the roster.","sources":[{"note_id":"n321a81ed4d","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Pete_Alonso"},{"note_id":"nd381d234f0","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"BaltimoreBaseball.com","author":"Rich Dubroff","url":"https://www.baltimorebaseball.com/sports/orioles-mlb/2026/11/17/alonso-caps-off-1st-orioles-season-silver-slugger-award-richdubroff/"},{"note_id":"n8017610645","article_date":"2026-10-19","source":"Sports Illustrated (On SI)","author":"Jason La Canfora","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/orioles/onsi/top-4-orioles-regression-candidates-in-2027-01m5b7fxktsp"},{"note_id":"n922ed3fefe","article_date":"2026-11-11","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jake Rill","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mike-elias-orioles-trade-rumors-2026-27-offseason"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Pete Alonso","team":"BAL","elig":"1B/CI"},"p2020362":{"text":"The 2026 AL Rookie of the Year, with 28 of 30 first-place votes, after leading the AL with 7.1 bWAR as a rookie shortstop and third baseman. He hit .278/.376/.430 with 18 homers, 72 RBI and 12 steals, and the plate discipline was elite: a 19.6% chase rate in the 98th percentile and a walk rate in the 94th. Detroit took him 37th overall in 2023 out of Monsignor Bonner High School in Pennsylvania, where he hit .530 and struck out twice in 25 senior games; scouts compared him to Chase Utley and put a 60 grade on the hit tool. Comerica caps the power for now, but Razzball expects more homers as he adds strength and calls him a multi-positional stud who can help a contender right away.","sources":[{"note_id":"nbda176f07f","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2026-mlb-rookie-of-the-year-voting-totals"},{"note_id":"ne947d31a33","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/ranking-2026-rookies-based-on-long-term-value"},{"note_id":"nb79ba5a154","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nb446d6d6bb","article_date":"2023-07-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jason Beck","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/kevin-mcgonigle-max-anderson-drafted-by-tigers-in-2023-mlb-draft"},{"note_id":"ne1c89fe177","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-third-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Kevin McGonigle","team":"DET","elig":"3B/SS/MI/CI"},"pf10637c":{"text":"He took a no-hitter into the eighth inning of ALDS Game 1 against the Yankees, capping a 16-5, 2.67 ERA, 0.91 WHIP season over 172 innings that included a 9-0, 1.86 run in 12 second-half starts. The road was long: the Rays drafted him 31st overall in 2017 but didn't sign him because he needed Tommy John surgery, Milwaukee took him in the sixth round in 2018, and Tampa Bay got him back in the 2021 Willy Adames trade. In 2023 he chose an internal brace over a third Tommy John, and he has answered with a second straight All-Star nod and June's AL Pitcher of the Month award. He made 30 starts and spent fewer than 45 days on the IL in 2026, triggering escalators, and the Rays exercised his $20 million option for 2027. Razzball calls him severely underrated, with a 2.83 ERA and 1.024 WHIP across 160 career games.","sources":[{"note_id":"n5814015aee","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Drew_Rasmussen"},{"note_id":"ne8376684c1","article_date":"2026-10-31","source":"CBS Sports (RotoWire)","author":"RotoWire Staff","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/rays-drew-rasmussen-rays-exercise-option/"},{"note_id":"na12f9a0645","article_date":"2026-10-15","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-100-dynasty-starting-pitchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Drew Rasmussen","team":"TBR","elig":"SP"},"p8fcd7f4":{"text":"He stole 30 bases for the second straight year, the first first baseman to do that since George Sisler in 1920-22, in a season that otherwise went sideways. A bad right wrist had him wincing after swings as far back as June; his slugging fell 112 points to a career-low .350, and he hit just two of his 10 homers after June 12. He had TFCC repair surgery on Oct. 3 and is expected to be fully recovered before spring training. CBS's Chris Towers flags a .241/.306/.326 line at T-Mobile Park and a career-worst .089 ISO, and warns of real bottom-out potential if the speed goes away. The 2015 No. 12 overall pick was the first free agent to sign after 2025, returning to Seattle on a five-year deal, and Bleacher Report projects him at first base batting fifth.","sources":[{"note_id":"nef990dcfcc","article_date":"2026-09-10","source":"Bleacher Report","author":"Joel Reuter","url":"https://bleacherreport.com/articles/25497410-predicting-every-mlb-teams-2027-opening-day-lineup"},{"note_id":"nd46640216b","article_date":"2026-10-05","source":"MLB.com","author":"Mariners Baseball Communications","url":"https://www.mlb.com/press-release/mariners-medical-update"},{"note_id":"nff817429b8","article_date":"2026-10-07","source":"MLB.com","author":"Daniel Kramer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/josh-naylor-dealt-with-injuries-amid-2026-offensive-struggles-for-mariners"},{"note_id":"n682e9b6092","article_date":"2026-10-09","source":"CBS Sports","author":"Chris Towers","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/fantasy-baseball-2027-first-base-outlook/"},{"note_id":"n7faf220ab8","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Josh_Naylor"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Josh Naylor","team":"SEA","elig":"1B/CI"},"p4ba2e16":{"text":"The fourth overall pick in 2023 out of Florida, once in the conversation with Paul Skenes and Dylan Crews for the top pick, he made the 2024 Opening Day roster, hit for the cycle that June 30 and was AL Player of the Month that September. His 2026 was a step back, .238/.333/.421 with 17 homers, around two IL stints for a right forearm strain and a hamstring strain, and he admitted late in the year he wasn't 100%. His exit velocity (91.4 to 88.6 mph), barrel rate (14.0% to 7.9%) and hard-hit rate (48.4% to 36.4%) all dropped, but the 11.7% walk rate held in the 84th percentile, and SI's Matt Postins blames the injuries for the slump. Texas wants him healthy for 162 games, so Chris Young would rather keep him in left field than move him to center, and would rather not platoon him.","sources":[{"note_id":"nd52448fca2","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Wyatt_Langford"},{"note_id":"n474893810e","article_date":"2026-10-16","source":"Sports Illustrated (On SI)","author":"Matt Postins","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/rangers/onsi/did-injuries-or-slump-hold-rangers-slugger-wyatt-langford-back-in-2026"},{"note_id":"n68e6860b41","article_date":"2026-11-16","source":"Dallas Sports Journal","author":"Jake Rogers","url":"https://dallassportsjournal.com/texas-rangers-wyatt-langford-left-field-center-field-search/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Wyatt Langford","team":"TEX","elig":"OF"},"p3672d6a":{"text":"Drew Rasmussen calls him \"the best pure hitter I've ever played with,\" and in 2026 the 35-year-old led the AL with 180 hits, batted .293 with 22 homers as Tampa Bay's primary DH and made the All-Star team. He defected from Cuba, signed with Cleveland in 2013 and came to the Rays in a December 2018 trade for Jake Bauers; in 2023 he became the first Ray ever to win a batting title. Reaching 500 plate appearances and finishing healthy turned his $10 million club option into a guaranteed $13 million for 2027. He has been at least 20% better than league average for five straight seasons, though he did hit just .213 in July. Kevin Cash says he'd pick him to start any inning, and Bleacher Report has him leading off at DH in 2027.","sources":[{"note_id":"n006c68ebc0","article_date":"2026-08-13","source":"MLB.com","author":"Theo DeRosa","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/yandy-diaz-leads-by-example-as-veteran-presence-for-rays"},{"note_id":"n010ed3f4ef","article_date":"2026-10-31","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Charlie Wright","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/10/rays-finalize-yandy-diazs-contract.html"},{"note_id":"nf03d1bb625","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Yandy_D%C3%ADaz"},{"note_id":"n7d3f0eb236","article_date":"2026-09-10","source":"Bleacher Report","author":"Joel Reuter","url":"https://bleacherreport.com/articles/25497410-predicting-every-mlb-teams-2027-opening-day-lineup"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Yandy Diaz","team":"TBR","elig":"1B/CI"},"p537390e":{"text":"He embraces the Ric Flair \"Woo!\" down to Flair's likeness on his cleats, and in 2025 he backed it up, going at least six innings with two or fewer walks in each of his first 16 starts on the way to his first All-Star team and fifth place in the AL Cy Young vote. A 2021 sixth-rounder out of Cal Poly, he debuted less than two years later, though his 2024 was broken up by four IL stints. His 2026 was a step back, 12-10 with a 4.01 ERA, 1.10 WHIP and 175 strikeouts in 173 innings, but RotoBaller's Will Brady pins much of it on a 66.2% strand rate against a 73.4% career mark. Right shoulder discomfort he called \"unusual\" pulled him from an Aug. 16 start, but he returned saying he felt \"100% confident,\" and he now has back-to-back 30-start seasons. Seattle plans to open 2027 with him in a rotation alongside Gilbert, Kirby, Hancock and Anderson.","sources":[{"note_id":"n08fc3337dd","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Bryan_Woo"},{"note_id":"neada15228e","article_date":"2026-11-09","source":"RotoBaller","author":"Will Brady","url":"https://www.rotoballer.com/player-news/bryan-woo-a-bounce-back-candidate-in-2027/1988485"},{"note_id":"n4e329d055f","article_date":"2026-08-25","source":"MLB.com","author":"Daniel Kramer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/bryan-woo-to-return-to-mariners-starting-rotation"},{"note_id":"n01d6c5bd78","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"MLB.com","author":"Daniel Kramer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mariners-plan-for-2026-27-offseason-after-maikel-garcia-trade"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Bryan Woo","team":"SEA","elig":"SP"},"pe315128":{"text":"He struck out 10 or more while walking one or fewer in five straight starts, a run only Chris Sale (2018) and Clayton Kershaw (2016) had matched since 1900, then fanned 12 in the ALDS Game 5 clincher. The breakthrough was control: after leading the majors with 83 walks in 2025, he cut his walk rate from 4.5 to 2.5 per nine and led the AL with 248 strikeouts. At 6-foot-6 with elite extension, he pairs a four-seamer that averaged 97.6 mph, and 99.1 in the postseason, with a sweeper that produced 100 strikeouts. The 3.76 ERA hides some uneven months, a 6.04 June and a 5.56 August, but the 3.48 FIP and a strikeout rate in the 97th percentile point up. Cleveland took him 23rd overall in 2021 out of East Carolina, and SI's Cade Cracas calls him a no-brainer as the Guardians' ace in 2027.","sources":[{"note_id":"nceb37469e9","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Gavin_Williams"},{"note_id":"ncc0c962445","article_date":"2026-10-14","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brent Maguire","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/gavin-williams-2026-postseason-analysis"},{"note_id":"n0538d9b7b8","article_date":"2026-10-15","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-100-dynasty-starting-pitchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nd4704645e9","article_date":"2026-10-30","source":"Sports Illustrated (On SI)","author":"Cade Cracas","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/guardians/onsi/how-the-guardians-starting-rotation-should-look-on-opening-day-in-2027-01m671rxtj9j"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Gavin Williams","team":"CLE","elig":"SP"},"p6bae4b5":{"text":"A week before his May 13 debut he hit a 479-foot homer, the longest by anyone at any level to that point of the season per Statcast, and he was called up after collecting hits in 12 consecutive plate appearances. The A's took him in the second round in 2022 out of a Silicon Valley high school, signing him away from a Texas commitment, and the A's fan got to debut for his hometown team. A right wrist problem sapped his power in 2025, but he said he was \"back to being free,\" and he got better as his rookie year went on: .261 with three homers in the first half, .289 with eight in the second, with 21 steals overall. He played solid center field and was a spark plug atop the lineup, and Athletics Nation expects him to start in center again in 2027, with Bleacher Report projecting him to lead off. He's still just 23.","sources":[{"note_id":"n77a4610de0","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Henry_Bolte"},{"note_id":"nf73521ebcf","article_date":"2026-05-13","source":"MLB.com","author":"Martín Gallegos","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/henry-bolte-athletics-callup"},{"note_id":"n42816b1608","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Corbin Young","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/hitters-who-gained-15-of-value-in-the-second-half-of-2026/"},{"note_id":"nfa03113064","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"Athletics Nation (SB Nation)","author":"Connor Ashford","url":"https://www.athleticsnation.com/athletics-news/111174/athletics-news-bolte-gets-one-fifth-place-vote-for-roty"},{"note_id":"n3f07df06a6","article_date":"2026-09-10","source":"Bleacher Report","author":"Joel Reuter","url":"https://bleacherreport.com/articles/25497410-predicting-every-mlb-teams-2027-opening-day-lineup"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Henry Bolte","team":"ATH","elig":"OF"},"p988af3f":{"text":"A Cohasset, Mass., Yankees fan who once wrote \"Yankees Rule\" on Fenway's Pesky Pole, he went from Dartmouth to a 12th-round pick in 2021 after a Yankees area scout followed him through pickup games and even a men's league. In 2026 he hit .264/.363/.534 with 41 homers, 105 runs and 97 RBI, building on a 26-homer 2025, and he was the majors' OPS leader in early May when a pickoff throw bruised his left hand, an injury that left him day to day. CBS's Chris Towers says he won't carry catcher eligibility into 2027 and seems unlikely ever to catch enough to get it back. SI's Michael Zeno sees him starting 150-plus games at first base or DH, with the Yankees invested in him as their first baseman of the future and Aaron Judge needing more DH days.","sources":[{"note_id":"n1bf45e28ef","article_date":"2025-04-24","source":"MLB.com","author":"Anthony Castrovince","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/ben-rice-path-to-key-piece-of-yankees-lineup"},{"note_id":"n13ddb20c1b","article_date":"2026-05-04","source":"MLB.com","author":"Bryan Hoch","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/ben-rice-out-of-starting-lineup-may-4"},{"note_id":"n56892ea10f","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-first-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n505a5fa666","article_date":"2026-10-09","source":"CBS Sports","author":"Chris Towers","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/fantasy-baseball-2027-first-base-outlook/"},{"note_id":"n220926df6c","article_date":"2026-10-20","source":"Sports Illustrated","author":"Michael Zeno","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/yankees/onsi/projecting-the-yankees-2027-opening-day-lineup-new-york-makes-changes-at-middle-infield-spots-01m55c7ggjnp"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Ben Rice","team":"NYY","elig":"C/1B/CI"},"p5d39d0a":{"text":"The unanimous 2026 AL MVP, with all 30 first-place votes, and only the second DH ever to win it. He also took the batting title at .316 and led the AL in OBP (.432), slugging (.601), OPS (1.033), walks (109) and extra-base hits (77), with career highs of 42 homers and 106 RBI in 692 plate appearances. That came a year after a broken right hand and a season-ending left ankle sprain held him to 48 games, and he said he got through 2026 without even minor injuries, pain or soreness. Houston got him as a minor leaguer in a lopsided trade, the way it once got Jeff Bagwell, and health was the only question on his bat until 2026 answered it.","sources":[{"note_id":"n3b4224fe7e","article_date":"2026-11-13","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian McTaggart","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/yordan-alvarez-wins-2026-al-mvp-award"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Yordan Alvarez","team":"HOU","elig":"OF"},"p82b3359":{"text":"Seattle took him 14th overall in 2018, the highest pick ever out of Stetson, after an 11-1, 2.52 ERA junior year with 157 strikeouts and 23 walks in 107 innings. Scouts liked an easy 94-97 mph fastball, four pitches and a ton of strikes, and the Mariners were surprised he was still there after some teams backed off over a midseason velocity dip. His ERA has drifted the wrong way, from 3.23 in 2024 to 3.44 in 2025 to 3.73 in 2026, though Razzball's Jakkers still isn't ready to give up on him. After the Maikel Garcia trade, Jerry Dipoto says Seattle is done dealing from the rotation and will open 2027 with Gilbert, Kirby, Woo, Hancock and Anderson.","sources":[{"note_id":"n67b513bcb1","article_date":"2018-06-04","source":"MLB.com","author":"Greg Johns","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mariners-draft-stetson-righty-logan-gilbert-c279778174"},{"note_id":"ne1de510b4f","article_date":"2026-10-15","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-100-dynasty-starting-pitchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nd5317183a6","article_date":"2026-11-19","source":"MLB.com","author":"Daniel Kramer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/mariners/news/mariners-plan-for-2026-27-offseason-after-maikel-garcia-trade"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Logan Gilbert","team":"SEA","elig":"SP"},"pb4d19ec":{"text":"He stayed durable in 2026, with a second straight season of 30-plus starts, double-digit wins and 201 strikeouts, but finished with a 4.20 ERA, the first above 4.00 of a career that began with the Mets in 2014. The contact got loud: a 45% hard-hit rate in the 4th percentile and 19 more earned runs than in 2025 in 10 fewer innings. 2027 is the final guaranteed year of the five-year, $185 million deal he signed with Texas before 2023, paying him $37 million before free agency in 2028. He vetoed a potential August trade because he wanted to stay in Arlington, and while SI's Matt Postins argues the ERA gives the Rangers reason to consider moving him this winter, for now he's a Ranger.","sources":[{"note_id":"n8affca47c3","article_date":"2026-11-16","source":"Sports Illustrated","author":"Matt Postins","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/rangers/onsi/jacob-degrom-s-rising-era-raises-hard-question-texas-rangers-2027"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Jacob deGrom","team":"TEX","elig":"SP"},"pba6785e":{"text":"The White Sox signed him Nov. 18 to a three-year, $52.5 million deal, part of GM Chris Getz's push to add starting pitching after a 2026 playoff run that leaned on Sean Burke as essentially the lone starter. Once a top Dodgers prospect, he missed all of 2024 to Tommy John recovery and then an esophageal tear, was traded to Boston at the 2025 deadline and has rebuilt his workload since, from 132 1/3 innings in 2025 to a career-high 145 2/3 in 2026. The results lagged after Milwaukee got him from St. Louis at the 2026 deadline, a 5.65 ERA in 10 starts and a 5.73 ERA in five postseason games while working as both a starter and reliever for the champions. Chicago signed him to start, and the contract says he'll get every chance to do it.","sources":[{"note_id":"n93d46ff1d0","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brent Maguire, Scott Merkin","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/dustin-may-white-sox-contract"}],"as_of":"2026-11-20","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-20","name":"Dustin May","team":"CHW","elig":"SP"},"p18b962a":{"text":"He led the Arizona Fall League with an .857 slugging percentage to win Hitter of the Week, one of just two 2026 draftees sent to the Fall League. Kansas City took him sixth overall in 2026 and signed him for $5,809,575, under the $7,746,100 slot, freeing money to sign Taylor Rabe and Jack Slightom. Ankle and hamstring injuries cost him 21 of Louisville's 57 games as a junior, but he hit .417/.491/.646 when he played, with more walks than strikeouts and 24 steals in 27 tries. Scouts like his advanced bat-to-ball skills and plus raw power, and scouting director Brian Bridges sees \"a little Ron Gant\" in the swing; the quibble is that he doesn't pull the ball in the air enough, and the Royals are only nudging his weight forward. A former catcher moved to the outfield because he was too athletic to stay behind the plate, he heads into his first full pro season as a hitter the Royals have believed in for a while.","sources":[{"note_id":"n7961d5f74a","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB Pipeline (X)","author":"MLB Pipeline","url":"https://x.com/MLBPipeline/status/2120185548102935003"},{"note_id":"n728a6ca5ea","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/royals-prospect-zion-rose-wrapping-up-pro-debut-in-arizona-fall-league"},{"note_id":"n4c8121d135","article_date":"2026-07-19","source":"MLB.com","author":"Anne Rogers","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/zion-rose-signs-contract-with-royals"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Zion Rose","team":"KCR","elig":"OF"},"p86b38f3":{"text":"He threw 4 1/3 perfect innings at Mesa on Oct. 21 to earn Arizona Fall League Pitcher of the Week, on an offseason assignment to add a curveball that drew a 41.2% whiff rate in his first two starts. Kansas City took the Texas A&M lefty 71st overall in 2025 and signed him for $1,161,200, essentially at slot. He looked like a breakout this spring, sitting 93-95 mph after 91-93 in college and posting a 2.04 ERA with 53 strikeouts and 14 walks in 39 2/3 innings between High-A and Double-A, until a shoulder issue the Royals called non-structural pulled him from a May 25 outing. The velocity isn't all the way back, averaging 92.4 mph in one Fall League outing, and rebuilding it is the next step. Roundtable's Grant Mona wouldn't rush him, but thinks he can push for a look in Kansas City at some point in 2027 if the fastball returns.","sources":[{"note_id":"n0f09acdef8","article_date":"2026-11-01","source":"Roundtable","author":"Grant Mona","url":"https://roundtable.io/sports/mlb/royals/news/justin-lamkin-explains-pitching-plan-as-fall-league-run-continues"},{"note_id":"n909bcdd3b5","article_date":"2026-10-16","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Sam Dykstra","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/royals-prospect-justin-lamkin-refining-breaking-ball-in-arizona-fall-league"},{"note_id":"n595f888f26","article_date":"2025-07-17","source":"Royals Review (SB Nation)","author":"Max Rieper","url":"https://www.royalsreview.com/2025/7/17/24468309/royals-2025-draft-sign-tracker-bonus-slot-pool"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Justin Lamkin","team":"KCR","elig":"SP"},"pf977fae":{"text":"Baltimore took him 22nd overall in 2024 and paid an over-slot $4 million for what Jim Callis says may have been the best all-around tools in that draft, but the swing-and-miss came with him: .175/.282/.298 with a 42% strikeout rate over his first 177 pro games. Then it clicked: after a July oblique strain at High-A, he quieted his hands with help from Maryland hitting coach Gerardo Caceres, earned a promotion and hit .288/.381/.616 at Double-A with five homers and 10 steals. He kept his strikeout rate at 25% in the Arizona Fall League and won its Defensive Player of the Year award with a league-leading three outfield assists, and some evaluators call him the best defensive outfielder in the minors. Birds Watcher calls 2027 make-or-break: if the contact gains hold, Callis sees 30-30 upside and Gold Glove potential, and if they slip, the shine comes off fast.","sources":[{"note_id":"n6244d90ac5","article_date":"2024-08-01","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com staff","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2024-draft-signings-and-bonus-tracker"},{"note_id":"nbe81b72211","article_date":"2026-11-07","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/orioles-prospect-vance-honeycutt-showing-improvement-in-arizona-fall-league"},{"note_id":"nb88d3ed45a","article_date":"2026-07-14","source":"CBS Sports (RotoWire)","author":"RotoWire Staff","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/orioles-vance-honeycutt-out-with-oblique-strain/"},{"note_id":"n76d8d74591","article_date":"2026-11-14","source":"MLB.com (AFL press release)","author":"Arizona Fall League","url":"https://www.mlb.com/press-release/press-release-arizona-fall-league-announces-award-winners-for-2026-season"},{"note_id":"n5678bcf9ab","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"Birds Watcher (On SI)","author":"Nicholas Stevens","url":"https://birdswatcher.com/3-orioles-prospects-entering-a-make-or-break-year-in-2027-01m6mvgyjhhz"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Vance Honeycutt","team":"BAL","elig":"OF"},"p7c99a58":{"text":"He hit .347/.480/.653 with five homers and nine steals in the Arizona Fall League, the league's second-best OPS, after a rough season at Double-A. Minnesota took him 33rd overall in 2024 with the pick it got for losing Sonny Gray, the highest selection in Louisiana-Lafayette history, and signed him for $2.4 million, a bit under slot. At Wichita he stayed healthy for 120 games but hit .221/.325/.352 as his contact rate fell more than five points, though his 41 steals gave him 107 over two years, the most among Twins minor leaguers. The 2025 Minor League Gold Glove winner at second base has also played short, third, left and center, and with Kaelen Culpepper and Brooks Lee locked into the infield, Zone Coverage sees his best path as a utility role by 2028.","sources":[{"note_id":"ned010d5b21","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"Zone Coverage","author":"Theo Tollefson","url":"https://zonecoverage.com/2026/mn-twins-news/kyle-debarge-is-showing-encouraging-signs-in-the-arizona-fall-league/"},{"note_id":"nd815474ec3","article_date":"2024-08-01","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com staff","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2024-draft-signings-and-bonus-tracker"},{"note_id":"n38cf197eaf","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jesse Borek","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/twins-prospect-kyle-debarge-showcasing-unique-style-in-fall-league"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Kyle DeBarge","team":"MIN","elig":"2B/MI"},"pd726c86":{"text":"In August 2025 he stretched his career-opening hitting streak to 11 games, two shy of the Twins' record, and won that same game with an 11th-inning walk-off homer against the Royals, on the way to AL Player of the Week. Minnesota took the hit-tool-first Arizona State second baseman 49th overall in 2023 for $1.5 million, under slot, betting his power would keep growing after his transfer from San Francisco. The health file is long: he played through a torn elbow ligament for much of 2024 before Tommy John surgery that August, a Kyle Hendricks sinker fractured his right ulna in his seventh big-league game in April 2025 (no surgery needed), and he had a torn ligament in his left thumb repaired on Oct. 1, 2025, yet he played 152 games in 2026 and hit .282/.374/.407 with 10 homers and 20 steals. He made what looks like a permanent move to the outfield this year and looked more comfortable as it went, and MLB.com's Matthew Leach expects him to start in a corner and play just about every day. SI's Alvin Garcia says the .374 OBP has earned him an extension conversation, just not a payday for power he hasn't shown yet.","sources":[{"note_id":"n6684a4ea02","article_date":"2025-08-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"Patrick Donnelly","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/luke-keaschall-walk-off-homer-in-extras-twins-win-series"},{"note_id":"nbbdcc36338","article_date":"2025-08-11","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/press-release/press-release-minnesota-s-luke-keaschall-named-american-league-player-of-the-week-presented-by-chevrolet-milwaukee-s-isaac-collins-named-national-league-player-of-the-week-presented-by-chevrolet"},{"note_id":"n882ecc7fc1","article_date":"2023-07-26","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com staff","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/twins-2023-draft-signings-tracker"},{"note_id":"na0746c210a","article_date":"2024-08-12","source":"Minnesota Sports Fan","author":"Ted Schwerzler","url":"https://www.minnesotasportsfan.com/minnesota-twins/minnesota-twins-news/minnesota-twins-prospect-news-luke-keaschall-exclusive-interview-torn-ucl-tommy-john/"},{"note_id":"n0e5ab20ecf","article_date":"2025-04-26","source":"MLB.com","author":"Patrick Donnelly","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/trevor-larnach-byron-buxton-homer-in-pablo-lopez-s-return-from-injury"},{"note_id":"n9b78a5f33a","article_date":"2025-04-28","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/royce-lewis-willi-castro-approaching-return-to-twins-lineup"},{"note_id":"n030bf3edc5","article_date":"2025-09-30","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/derek-falvey-expects-joe-ryan-pablo-lopez-on-twins-in-2026"},{"note_id":"n1896a3b938","article_date":"2026-10-12","source":"Sports Illustrated (On SI)","author":"Alvin Garcia","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/twins/onsi/twins-should-test-luke-keaschall-extension-price-now"},{"note_id":"n72419d6bc1","article_date":"2026-11-09","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/twins-outfield-outlook-for-2027-season"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Luke Keaschall","team":"MIN","elig":"2B/MI/OF"},"pe225715":{"text":"He hit four homers in his first three big-league games in March, something only Trevor Story had done before him. Cleveland took the James Madison two-way player 16th overall in 2022, his school's first first-rounder, even after he broke his foot that April, and the injuries kept coming: another broken foot, sports hernia surgery and a broken hamate. In 2026 he finally stayed on the field, playing 133 games after a previous pro high of 57 and hitting .287, fifth in the AL, with 30 doubles, 16 homers and an .820 OPS, and he won an AL Silver Slugger, the first rookie in franchise history to do it, while finishing sixth in Rookie of the Year voting. His only IL trip was 11 games for a fractured right rib in June. GM Mike Chernoff wants him playing even more, in the outfield every day with no lingering injury concern, and SI's Bryce Gatten calls him the only sure thing in Cleveland's outfield.","sources":[{"note_id":"n9fed1d5cb6","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Chase_DeLauter"},{"note_id":"n434ac00fbe","article_date":"2026-11-09","source":"MLB.com","author":"Tim Stebbins","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/chase-delauter-silver-slugger-award-nomination-follows-durable-season"},{"note_id":"n572791784e","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/press-release/rf-chase-delauter-awarded-al-silver-slugger-award"},{"note_id":"n06f1756519","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2026-mlb-rookie-of-the-year-voting-totals"},{"note_id":"n95ee0471ab","article_date":"2026-11-08","source":"Sports Illustrated (On SI)","author":"Bryce Gatten","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/guardians/onsi/the-guardians-have-a-difficult-decision-to-make-about-their-outfield-this-offseason-01m6y9tx7gv3"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Chase DeLauter","team":"CLE","elig":"OF"},"pf0ce583":{"text":"On April 16 he carried a no-hitter into the ninth inning against Baltimore, chasing Cleveland's first since Len Barker's 1981 perfect game, before Leody Taveras broke it up. A 2022 second-round pick out of Florida State, he came to camp with nothing guaranteed and finished second in AL Rookie of the Year voting and as a Cy Young finalist, with a 2.56 ERA, 193 strikeouts and 6.3 bWAR over 186 1/3 innings in 32 starts. He allowed three or fewer earned runs in 29 of those starts and joined Score (1955) and Stigman (1960) as the only Cleveland rookie pitchers named All-Stars. The worry is workload: he threw exactly 200 innings including the playoffs after 149 the year before, and Covering the Corner's Quincy Wheeler ties his 3.95 postseason ERA and a 3-degree arm-angle drop to fatigue. Vogt calls him \"uber competitive,\" and Wheeler expects another big year from the rookie who anchored the rotation.","sources":[{"note_id":"n6ad8122a1c","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Parker_Messick"},{"note_id":"n17152f8bf0","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"Tim Stebbins","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/parker-messick-second-al-rookie-of-the-year-award-2026"},{"note_id":"n2806eaa8f5","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2026-mlb-rookie-of-the-year-voting-totals"},{"note_id":"n236ae05bbb","article_date":"2026-11-16","source":"Covering the Corner (SB Nation)","author":"Quincy Wheeler","url":"https://www.coveringthecorner.com/cleveland-guardians-analysis/76767/parker-messick-season-in-review"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Parker Messick","team":"CLE","elig":"SP"},"p8c38fb2":{"text":"On Sept. 13 he threw an immaculate inning against Kansas City, striking out Loftin, Witt and Caglianone, Boston's first since Chris Sale in 2021 and its first in a first inning since Pedro Martinez in 2002. Boston took him 50th overall in 2024 out of TCU, a former two-way player who hit 13 homers in a season at Wichita State, and wanted him only on the mound, even though his draft report called him polarizing, a possible multi-inning reliever. He opened 2026 projected as a back-end starter, then became one of the AL's best after Crochet's injury: a 3.03 ERA, 171 strikeouts, 40 walks and a 1.10 WHIP over 148 2/3 innings, good for third in the Rookie of the Year vote. Razzball notes he fixed the 4.4 walks per nine from his 2025 cameo and got better as the season went on; the one caution is a leash that often stopped around 90 pitches. NBC Sports Boston's Justin Leger has him second in the 2027 rotation behind Crochet.","sources":[{"note_id":"n16253604d4","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Payton_Tolle"},{"note_id":"nb446e74f84","article_date":"2026-10-21","source":"NESN","author":"Daniel Mader","url":"https://nesn.com/boston-red-sox/news/red-sox-year-review-2026-payton-tolle-season/4585a26d2daa31e1fb50a68b"},{"note_id":"n0045004bf2","article_date":"2024-07-15","source":"Boston.com","author":"Khari A. Thompson","url":"https://www.boston.com/sports/boston-red-sox/2024/07/15/red-sox-payton-tolle-2024-mlb-draft-comments/"},{"note_id":"necfba09994","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2026-mlb-rookie-of-the-year-voting-totals"},{"note_id":"na948e5e559","article_date":"2026-11-19","source":"NBC Sports Boston","author":"Justin Leger","url":"https://www.nbcboston.com/mlb/boston-red-sox/roster-reset-offseason-depth-chart-2027/4051654/"},{"note_id":"n67c18f23ee","article_date":"2026-10-15","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-100-dynasty-starting-pitchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Payton Tolle","team":"BOS","elig":"SP"},"p6b44680":{"text":"He opened 2026 with three homers in the first two games, matched in A's history only by Al Simmons, and was an All-Star with 23 homers and a .263/.332/.497 line in 93 games before tearing his right meniscus. Atlanta took him ninth overall in 2019, and he was the key piece Oakland got back in the Matt Olson trade; he has since had a three-homer game as a catcher and another in his first career leadoff start, on the way to 31 homers in 2025. Surgery on July 28 was expected to end his season, but he was back Sept. 12 as the DH, went homerless in 48 September at-bats and has not yet returned behind the plate. Kotsay calls him a core run producer the club will rely on, but with two years of team control left, Ken Rosenthal reports he's drawing trade interest, so his team could change before draft day.","sources":[{"note_id":"nf3c4f071e1","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Shea_Langeliers"},{"note_id":"n1a151bdeb1","article_date":"2026-09-13","source":"MLB.com","author":"Theo DeRosa","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/shea-langeliers-activated-off-injured-list-by-athletics"},{"note_id":"n0f00ac1e41","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"Roundtable","author":"Jason Burke","url":"https://roundtable.io/sports/mlb/athletics/news/shea-langeliers-could-sit-atop-the-catching-market-if-the-as-decide-to-trade-him"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Shea Langeliers","team":"ATH","elig":"C"},"p83239ef":{"text":"He tied a Mets record with nine RBIs on April 28, 2025, and was the longest-tenured Met when New York traded him to Texas for Marcus Semien. The first first-round pick ever from Wyoming, a state without high school baseball, he went 13th overall in 2011 for a reported $2.1 million and later signed an eight-year, $162 million deal to stay with the Mets. His first Rangers season was solid but quiet, .260/.324/.405 with 14 homers and 62 RBI, both five-year lows, though his 92.1-mph average exit velocity and 50.4% hard-hit rate sat in the 91st and 93rd percentiles. The concerns are a career-worst 29% chase rate and a history of injury-limited seasons; a June crash into the Rogers Centre wall sprained his left AC joint and left him day to day. Texas is tied to him for four more seasons, and SI's Matt Postins calls his corner outfield spot safe for 2027.","sources":[{"note_id":"n5d979c2981","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Brandon_Nimmo"},{"note_id":"n99469fa683","article_date":"2026-10-18","source":"Sports Illustrated (On SI)","author":"Matt Postins","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/rangers/onsi/did-texas-rangers-get-what-they-needed-from-brandon-nimmo-in-2026"},{"note_id":"nd833f32c9f","article_date":"2026-06-30","source":"MLB.com","author":"Henry Palattella","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/brandon-nimmo-has-sprained-ac-joint"},{"note_id":"n38dac91c0f","article_date":"2026-10-30","source":"Sports Illustrated (On SI)","author":"Matt Postins","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/rangers/onsi/projecting-texas-rangers-2027-opening-day-roster-before-free-agency-begins"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Brandon Nimmo","team":"TEX","elig":"OF"},"p15b40ae":{"text":"He hit for the cycle on his own bobblehead day at Target Field on July 12, 2025, the year he hit 30 homers and won a Silver Slugger. The No. 2 pick in 2012, one spot after Carlos Correa, he signed for $6 million out of Georgia's Appling County High, where he hit .513, stole 38 bases and went 10-0 on the mound. Hip pain dogged him from May in 2026: he was at .268/.325/.573 heading into July and finished with 25 homers and a 128 wRC+ in 93 games, but hit .172 with no homers after his first IL stint and had arthroscopic hip surgery Sept. 18. The Twins say he's \"feeling great,\" with a Dec. 21 exam set to clear him for baseball activity, and Jeremy Zoll says they want him in Minnesota, where he's due $15 million in each of the next two seasons. MLB.com's Matthew Leach sees him as the primary center fielder, with more DH time to protect the hip.","sources":[{"note_id":"ne1b7b02132","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Byron_Buxton"},{"note_id":"nab1f9591d4","article_date":"2026-10-02","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Charlie Wright","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/10/byron-buxton-ready-for-2027-twins-not-planning-to-trade-him.html"},{"note_id":"nffb518ac3d","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/twins-gm-jeremy-zoll-updates-byron-buxton-walker-jenkins-more-players-injuries"},{"note_id":"n1836c3ecc1","article_date":"2026-11-09","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/twins-outfield-outlook-for-2027-season"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Byron Buxton","team":"MIN","elig":"OF"},"pd70820f":{"text":"He became the first player in White Sox history with 30 doubles, 30 homers, 20 steals and 100 runs in a season, finishing his first All-Star year at .250/.363/.474 with 33 homers, 106 runs, 101 walks, 22 steals and 5.4 bWAR. That's a long way from 2024, when Chicago got him from the Dodgers at the deadline in a three-team, eight-player deal and he hit .104 in 42 games; MLB.com's Scott Merkin, once a skeptic, now calls him one of the best third basemen in the game. CBS's Chris Towers sees a breakout built to last: his walk rate jumped from 9.8% to 14.3% with no rise in strikeouts, he hit the ball harder, and he was good against lefties and righties and in both halves, with the only knock being that he hasn't done it twice. He played 159 games, and his one scare, a CJ Van Eyk sinker off the hand in July, didn't cost him a game; manager Will Venable said the pitch had nothing to do with his exit. The open question is position: he played 140 games at third, his first everyday run there, with Statcast grading his range well below average, and Chris Getz has mentioned an infield surplus, though the White Sox haven't planned a move.","sources":[{"note_id":"nf63d25c415","article_date":"2026-09-23","source":"MLB.com","author":"Scott Merkin","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/miguel-vargas-has-grown-into-star-player-with-white-sox"},{"note_id":"nf9b7c292b9","article_date":"2026-10-27","source":"Just Baseball","author":"Adrian White","url":"https://www.justbaseball.com/mlb/miguel-vargas-became-the-heart-of-the-white-sox-turnaround/"},{"note_id":"n45988c8f46","article_date":"2026-10-09","source":"CBS Sports","author":"Chris Towers","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/fantasy-baseball-2027-first-base-outlook/"},{"note_id":"n9f9be5872f","article_date":"2026-07-18","source":"MLB.com","author":"Zach Worden","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/miguel-vargas-exits-after-being-hit-by-pitch-on-left-hand"}],"as_of":"2026-11-21","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Miguel Vargas","team":"CHW","elig":"1B/3B/CI"},"p9d33a01":{"text":"He hit .262/.360/.447 with 25 homers, 27 doubles and 94 RBIs in 155 games, walking 10.8 percent of the time and cutting his strikeout rate to a career-low 13.4 percent, and Houston exercised his $13.35 million club option. The Cubs signed him before the 2016 season, and after stops in Detroit, Tampa Bay and Chicago he arrived in the December 2024 Kyle Tucker trade with Hayden Wesneski and Cam Smith; his extreme pull, fly-ball swing is a natural fit for Daikin Park's short porch in left. A hamstring injury in July 2025 nearly ended that season until he chose rehab over surgery, and he stayed on the field through 2026, though defensive metrics were hard on him at third. With Carlos Correa due back healthy, the Astros again have five infielders for four spots, and Steve Adams of MLB Trade Rumors says whether they trade someone — perhaps Paredes himself — will decide where he lines up.","sources":[{"note_id":"n6121c7530d","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Isaac_Paredes"},{"note_id":"n91c606188d","article_date":"2025-09-19","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian McTaggart","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/isaac-paredes-activated-off-injured-list-by-astros"},{"note_id":"n16f053bd23","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Steve Adams","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/11/astros-exercise-club-option-on-isaac-paredes.html"},{"note_id":"n2445ff3be0","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian McTaggart","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jake-meyers-outrighted-by-astros"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Isaac Paredes","team":"HOU","elig":"1B/3B/CI"},"p8aa4248":{"text":"He threw a complete-game no-hitter against Tampa Bay on May 10, 2022, the 12th in franchise history, and later that season added an immaculate inning, becoming just the third pitcher to do both in the same year, joining Sandy Koufax and Mike Fiers. The Angels took the Louisville lefty 10th overall in 2020, betting on a command-first fastball. After a 2025 relief year ended with left elbow inflammation in September, he bounced back as a starter in 2026: a 3.36 ERA and 211 strikeouts over 185 innings, good for sixth in AL Cy Young voting. He has two years of arbitration control left before free agency after 2028, and while Feinsand of MLB.com has again named him a trade candidate, Feinsand also reported the Angels are expected to keep him at least until a new general manager is hired.","sources":[{"note_id":"n865c9538a5","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Reid_Detmers"},{"note_id":"neb29fe89be","article_date":"2025-09-12","source":"MLB.com","author":"Rhett Bollinger","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/reid-detmers-on-injured-list-with-elbow-injury"},{"note_id":"n4c0c8aae2e","article_date":"2026-11-16","source":"Roundtable (Halos Roundtable)","author":"Matt Wadleigh","url":"https://roundtable.io/sports/mlb/angels/news/reid-detmers-named-as-trade-candidate-for-angels-once-again"},{"note_id":"n8d56c2c7ed","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"Angels On SI","author":"Jeff Joiner","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/angels/onsi/how-dustin-may-s-52-5m-contract-reshapes-the-angels-plans-for-reid-detmers-and-yusei-kikuchi-01m7nd3zezdv"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Reid Detmers","team":"LAA","elig":"SP"},"p7abec53":{"text":"Minnesota took the Cal Poly shortstop eighth overall in 2022, going slightly over the $5,442,400 slot to sign him for $5.675 million, and the Twins were glad a switch-hitter with bat-to-ball skill and pop from both sides was still there. He is named after Brooks Robinson and played at Cal Poly for his father, Larry, after a college ligament tear in his knee and a hamstring injury that required an unusual surgery; a 2024 start-of-season injury delayed his first full look. In 2026 he played a career-high 156 games, hit .253/.300/.428 with 24 homers and 83 RBIs, and started 110 games at third, where his .966 fielding percentage beat the league average and made him a Gold Glove finalist. He also played 48 games at short, but after the Royce Lewis trade, manager Derek Shelton said, \"Brooks [Lee] is going to play third,\" with Kaelen Culpepper expected at short.","sources":[{"note_id":"na77ed74911","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Brooks_Lee"},{"note_id":"n37c7e1cda4","article_date":"2022-07","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com staff (citing Jim Callis)","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/twins-2022-draft-signings-tracker"},{"note_id":"n7b4083177c","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nc6c4867d89","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Twins On SI","author":"Cade Lalim","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/twins/onsi/brooks-lee-s-move-to-third-base-perfectly-fits-twins-long-term-plans"},{"note_id":"n101fdf6ac7","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/royce-lewis-traded-from-twins-to-dodgers"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Brooks Lee","team":"MIN","elig":"2B/3B/SS/MI/CI"},"p7ebff20":{"text":"On June 4, 2025, he hit the shortest walk-off homer of the Statcast era, barely 308 feet around the Pesky Pole. Boston signed him out of Curacao on July 2, 2017, and on April 8, 2024, he agreed to an eight-year, $50 million extension, with $44 million remaining through 2031 including the buyout of a 2032 club option. In 2026, after an early Roman Anthony injury put him in center every day, he hit .283/.319/.442 with 16 homers and a career-high 70 RBIs, made his first All-Star team and won a second straight Gold Glove, with 17 outs above average and a major-league-best 10 five-star catches. No injury turned up in the notes, and Zach Coe has him back as the everyday center fielder in 2027, even as the outfield remains crowded on paper with Anthony, Wilyer Abreu, Jarren Duran and Masataka Yoshida.","sources":[{"note_id":"n0ed9659eae","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Ceddanne_Rafaela"},{"note_id":"n9ad9272299","article_date":"2026-10-09","source":"Roundtable (Red Sox Roundtable)","author":"Zach Coe","url":"https://roundtable.io/sports/mlb/red-sox/news/boston-red-sox-roster-way-too-early-look-at-2027-lineup"},{"note_id":"n1475f3ee75","article_date":"2026-10-21","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Darragh McDonald","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/10/offseason-outlook-boston-red-sox-16.html"},{"note_id":"n71e7ed4aa8","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"MLB.com","author":"Ian Browne","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/caleb-durbin-wilyer-abreu-ceddanne-rafaela-win-gold-glove-awards"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Ceddanne Rafaela","team":"BOS","elig":"2B/MI/OF"},"p66c1494":{"text":"After Tommy John surgery in March 2025 cost him the entire season, he was activated on May 22 and threw six scoreless innings against Tampa Bay, then went 10-9 with a 3.61 ERA, 131 strikeouts and a career-best 4.8 percent walk rate in 127 innings over 22 starts. The first overall pick in 2011, he signed a $324 million deal with the Yankees, then the richest free-agent contract for a pitcher, and is owed $36 million in 2027. He held his fastball at 96.7 mph, in the 84th percentile, though hard-contact and ground-ball rates sat at the bottom of the league, and on Sept. 6 he became the first pitcher in major league history to allow home runs to the first three batters of a game for a second time. The Yankees tabbed him for ALDS Game 1, and Theo DeRosa calls him the elder statesman of the 2027 rotation, provided the velocity holds.","sources":[{"note_id":"n2a874898a0","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Gerrit_Cole"},{"note_id":"n7d82d8eaf2","article_date":"2026-10-02","source":"MLB.com","author":"Bryan Hoch","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/gerrit-cole-set-to-start-alds-game-1-for-yankees"},{"note_id":"n85d54bea45","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Pinstripe Alley","author":"Kevin Winterhalt","url":"https://www.pinstripealley.com/yankees-roster/214014/yankees-report-cards-gerrit-cole-pitcher-injury-walks-strikeouts-home-runs"},{"note_id":"n624c006aff","article_date":"2026-11-08","source":"MLB.com","author":"Theo DeRosa","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/yankees-2027-rotation-outlook"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Gerrit Cole","team":"NYY","elig":"SP"},"p6aa8b9e":{"text":"Tampa Bay signed him out of Tijuana as an amateur on July 2, 2015, and for years he was a contact hitter with little power, totaling five home runs from 2016 through 2021. The bat woke up at Durham in 2022, when he was the International League Player of the Year, and in 2025 he made the All-Star team before a left-wrist fracture on Aug. 1 cut the year short; he still finished at .316 with 14 homers and a 146 OPS+ in 106 games. In 2026 he hit .274/.358/.434 with 22 homers, and no injury from that season turned up in the notes. After the Rays' World Series run, Adam Berry lists him with Junior Caminero and Yandy Diaz as one of the best hitters in the AL, all under club control for 2027.","sources":[{"note_id":"n3e420d6766","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Jonathan_Aranda"},{"note_id":"n4fb7b1a2ea","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"New Baseball Media","author":"Chris Studley","url":"https://newbaseballmedia.com/2026-mlb-season-recap-tampa-bay-rays/"},{"note_id":"na8b69613c0","article_date":"2026-11-19","source":"MLB.com","author":"Adam Berry","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/rays-areas-of-improvement"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Jonathan Aranda","team":"TBR","elig":"1B/CI"},"p8604871":{"text":"Seattle took him third overall in 2025, hours after he shut out Coastal Carolina in the College World Series finals to win Most Outstanding Player for LSU, and signed him for $8.8 million. He went 10-1 with a 1.06 ERA at Double-A Arkansas, with 135 strikeouts, 13 walks and 47 hits allowed in 93 1/3 innings, the lowest ERA by a minor leaguer with at least 90 innings since 1974, and on Aug. 22 he became the first player from his draft class to reach the majors. In seven big-league starts he went 2-2 with a 3.79 ERA, 32 strikeouts and 2.3 walks per nine over 35 2/3 innings, closing with 10 1/3 scoreless innings in his last two starts, and he did not miss time after the club capped him at 80 pitches in the minors. He won MLB Pipeline's Pitching Prospect of the Year, still has rookie eligibility, and Daniel Kramer has him in Seattle's Opening Day rotation with Logan Gilbert, George Kirby, Bryan Woo and Emerson Hancock.","sources":[{"note_id":"n3ff9327316","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Kade_Anderson"},{"note_id":"n6e1db79208","article_date":"2026-09-29","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Ben Weinrib","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mariners-kade-anderson-named-2026-pitching-prospect-of-the-year"},{"note_id":"ncebead4ebe","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/kade-anderson-2027-fantasy-outlook/"},{"note_id":"n92cceefc4e","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"},{"note_id":"ne49eb98c5b","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"MLB.com","author":"Daniel Kramer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mariners-plan-for-2026-27-offseason-after-maikel-garcia-trade"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Kade Anderson","team":"SEA","elig":"SP"},"p873be07":{"text":"He was the MVP of the 2026 World Baseball Classic, hitting .385 with a homer and seven RBIs as Venezuela won the title. Kansas City signed him out of Venezuela on July 27, 2016, and in December 2025 he signed a five-year, $57.5 million extension, with a 2031 club option, that could reach $85 million; his 2027 salary is $7 million. Injuries limited him to 82 games in 2026, a left-hand muscle strain that cost him 46 games and a left hamstring strain that cost him 21 more, and he hit .268 with three homers after a 2025 All-Star and Gold Glove year. On Nov. 18 the Mariners acquired him in a six-player trade, with James McArthur and L.P. Langevin going to Kansas City for Logan Evans, Jose Ferrer and Bryce Miller, and Daniel Kramer calls him Seattle's third baseman of the future.","sources":[{"note_id":"n1018c52b5d","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Maikel_Garcia"},{"note_id":"nb5b5b087d5","article_date":"2026-09-16","source":"MLB.com","author":"Steve Schaeffer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/maikel-garcia-activated-from-injured-list-lucas-erceg-recalled"},{"note_id":"n9389e1f858","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"MLB.com","author":"Daniel Kramer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mariners-plan-for-2026-27-offseason-after-maikel-garcia-trade"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Maikel Garcia","team":"SEA","elig":"3B/CI"},"pe0cfab4":{"text":"The Athletics took him 26th overall in 2020, after his father Steve went sixth overall in 1993, and he passed up UCLA to sign; in December 2025 he agreed to a club-record seven-year, $86 million deal. He hit both home runs in the club's first game as \"The Athletics\" on March 27, 2025, and briefly led the majors with eight. In 2026 he hit .245 with an .810 OPS, 19 homers and 56 RBIs in 109 games before season-ending left-hip surgery on Aug. 21, and the club expects him to be ready for spring training. Jason Burke already has him down as the everyday left fielder for 2027.","sources":[{"note_id":"nf32b7520f6","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Tyler_Soderstrom"},{"note_id":"n037d2341f0","article_date":"2026-08-21","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com staff","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/athletics-injuries-and-roster-moves"},{"note_id":"n21457274ce","article_date":"2026-09-29","source":"Roundtable (Athletics Roundtable)","author":"Jason Burke","url":"https://roundtable.io/sports/mlb/athletics/news/where-the-as-plan-to-use-zack-gelof-in-2027"},{"note_id":"n4c322e8c5c","article_date":"2026-10-03","source":"Athletics On SI","author":"Andrew L. Ferguson Jr","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/athletics/onsi/las-vegas-projecting-2027-outfield-player-stat-lines"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Tyler Soderstrom","team":"ATH","elig":"1B/CI/OF"},"p8b0d590":{"text":"As Team Italy's captain at the 2026 World Baseball Classic, he hit three homers against Mexico on March 11, the first three-homer game in tournament history. Kansas City took him in the 11th round in 2019 out of Old Dominion, and on Jan. 30, 2026, he signed a two-year extension. A torn labrum in his right shoulder ended his 2023, and 2026 brought more time on the shelf: surgery on a right hamate fracture, then a return to the injured list on July 29 with right wrist soreness before he was reinstated on Aug. 18. He still finished strong, batting .222 before the All-Star break and .294 after it, with 11 of his home runs in the second half, and he was a Gold Glove finalist; Feinsand of MLB.com has named him a trade candidate, which would open first base for Jac Caglianone, while Dan Szymborski calls him a solid starter if he stays.","sources":[{"note_id":"ne84ec7c9c7","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Vinnie_Pasquantino"},{"note_id":"n6981d35118","article_date":"2026-07-29","source":"MLB.com","author":"Anne Rogers","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/vinnie-pasquantino-injured-list-right-wrist"},{"note_id":"n4dee5f7e0f","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Corbin Young","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/hitters-who-gained-15-of-value-in-the-second-half-of-2026/"},{"note_id":"n459e47c4d4","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"Royals On SI","author":"Brian Sparks","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/royals/onsi/could-this-be-the-end-of-vinnie-pasquantino-s-time-with-the-royals-brian3"},{"note_id":"n2405264ec3","article_date":"2026-11-19","source":"FanGraphs","author":"Dan Szymborski","url":"https://blogs.fangraphs.com/2027-zips-projections-kansas-city-royals/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Vinnie Pasquantino","team":"KCR","elig":"1B/CI"},"pc7a2ea2":{"text":"He homered on the first pitch of his first major-league at-bat in 2016 and won a World Series with the Cubs that year, then signed a five-year, $87.5 million deal with St. Louis. After a move to first base in 2025, the Cardinals sent him to Boston that December with $8 million toward his remaining salary for Blake Aida, Hunter Dobbins and Yhoiker Fajardo, and he is owed $24.5 million through 2027, including the buyout of a 2028 club option. As the Red Sox' starting first baseman he hit .275 with 27 homers, 81 RBIs, a .386 on-base percentage and a .516 slugging percentage in 130 games, made the All-Star team and led the club in homers, RBIs, on-base percentage and slugging, though a September hamstring strain and a Sept. 17 pitch off his right hand cost him time. Justin Leger, citing Chris Cotillo, says Boston wants him healthy for 162 games and plans to keep him as the primary first baseman, with Jake Bauers cycling in at DH to get him off his feet.","sources":[{"note_id":"n6fd14af9ec","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Willson_Contreras"},{"note_id":"n39247dc528","article_date":"2026-09-27","source":"MLB.com","author":"Joey Johnston","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/willson-contreras-returns-to-lineup-might-play-in-wild-card-series"},{"note_id":"nb4840e1e7c","article_date":"2026-10-21","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Darragh McDonald","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/10/offseason-outlook-boston-red-sox-16.html"},{"note_id":"nff1889d333","article_date":"2026-11-13","source":"NBC Sports Boston","author":"Justin Leger","url":"https://www.nbcboston.com/mlb/boston-red-sox/jake-bauers-signing-willson-contreras-first-base/4049152/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Willson Contreras","team":"BOS","elig":"1B/CI"},"pbd6b881":{"text":"He stretched a hitting streak to 24 games, the sixth-longest in Athletics history, batting .351 with five homers and seven doubles during the run, until Matt Chapman accidentally stepped on his hand and sent him to the injured list. Oakland took him 60th overall in 2021 out of Virginia and signed him for a full-slot $1,157,400. After a 2025 wrecked by hamate surgery and a season-ending shoulder dislocation, he hit .256 with a 114 wRC+, 22 homers and 16 steals in 111 games in 2026, then missed nearly a month with a right-knee laceration from a fence collision in Detroit. He started 66 games at third, the most in a season under Kotsay, and saved nine defensive runs there; Kotsay says no one has taken the position quite like he did, and Jason Burke has him preparing as the primary third baseman for 2027, with some outfield work for versatility.","sources":[{"note_id":"ndc2f164f54","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Zack_Gelof"},{"note_id":"n002e2c850e","article_date":"2021-07-25","source":"MLB.com","author":"Martín Gallegos","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/athletics-sign-second-round-draft-pick-zack-gelof"},{"note_id":"n6e2e907bc8","article_date":"2026-09-29","source":"Roundtable (Athletics Roundtable)","author":"Jason Burke","url":"https://roundtable.io/sports/mlb/athletics/news/where-the-as-plan-to-use-zack-gelof-in-2027"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Zack Gelof","team":"ATH","elig":"2B/3B/MI/CI/OF"},"pd3bf6d0":{"text":"He won the 2024 Silver Slugger as the AL's designated hitter after hitting .293 with 39 homers and 112 RBIs, and on Jan. 6, 2025, he signed a five-year, $60 million extension with an option for a sixth season. The Twins took him 35th overall in 2017 out of Mississippi State, the SEC Player of the Year as a junior after he had passed on them as a 38th-rounder the year before, and Oakland claimed him off waivers after stops in San Diego and Kansas City. His 2026 ended in July with knee surgery; he hit .200 with a .670 OPS and a 32 percent strikeout rate in 48 games, still barreling the ball 15.4 percent of the time, and a late-season hitting and running progression for a left-knee bone bruise has him expected back for spring training. Jon Anderson of MLB Data Warehouse says he will be DH-only in 2027 after playing just four games in the outfield, and that the best version of him still has to start with health.","sources":[{"note_id":"n90ad583545","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Brent_Rooker"},{"note_id":"n41e5c68f70","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com staff","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/athletics-injuries-and-roster-moves"},{"note_id":"n21388232ae","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"MLB Data Warehouse","author":"Jon Anderson","url":"https://www.mlbdatawarehouse.com/2027-team-previews-athletics/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Brent Rooker","team":"ATH","elig":"OF"},"pa5aa3c8":{"text":"He tied for the major-league lead with 41 saves, converting all but five of his chances, and put up a 1.95 ERA, a 0.96 WHIP and a career-high 107 strikeouts, which led American League relievers, in 74 innings over 69 appearances. Cleveland signed the Abbotsford, B.C., right-hander for a minimal bonus on June 18, 2020, after the pandemic draft shrank to five rounds and left the Hawaii pitcher undrafted, a year after he had turned down the Twins as a 16th-rounder. He took over as closer when Emmanuel Clase was suspended in 2025, and in his first full year in the role he made the All-Star team, finished second for AL Reliever of the Year, landed on the All-MLB Second Team and pitched more than one inning 13 times. A splitter he began working on in 2023, thrown at 87.1 mph with the lowest spin rate in the majors not counting knuckleballs, held opponents to a .092 average and a 45.3 percent whiff rate, and Vogt says there's nobody he feels better giving the ball to in the ninth.","sources":[{"note_id":"nf90d999a9c","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Cade_Smith"},{"note_id":"n6e3615e7be","article_date":"2026-10-02","source":"FanGraphs","author":"David Laurila","url":"https://blogs.fangraphs.com/cade-smith-found-the-sweet-spot-on-a-lethal-low-spin-splitter/"},{"note_id":"n85736e4867","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"MLB.com","author":"Tim Stebbins","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/parker-messick-finishes-third-in-al-cy-young-award-voting"},{"note_id":"ne2559adc2f","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"MLB.com","author":"Tim Stebbins","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/cade-smith-2026-all-mlb"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Cade Smith","team":"CLE","elig":"RP"},"p9a2ef80":{"text":"The Cubs took him 14th overall in 2024 out of Florida State, where he hit .387, and signed him for a full-slot $5,070,700, then included him with Isaac Paredes and Hayden Wesneski in the Kyle Tucker trade to Houston. He made the 2025 Opening Day roster, the first player from that draft to debut, after working in right field with Michael Brantley. In 2026 he started 139 games in right, was a Gold Glove finalist for a second straight year, and hit 21 home runs, up from nine, with his barrel rate jumping to 11.2 percent and his bat speed up 3.4 mph, the largest increase of any qualified hitter; he also hit .210 with a .279 on-base percentage and struck out 26.1 percent of the time. A foul ball off his foot in June cost him only a day, and Thomas Heiberger calls 2027 a key inflection point for the everyday right fielder in his age-24 season.","sources":[{"note_id":"n9864fc973c","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Cam_Smith"},{"note_id":"n6b1a52ce5b","article_date":"2024-07-20","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jordan Bastian","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/cam-smith-agrees-to-deal-with-cubs"},{"note_id":"n0d93cbf9da","article_date":"2026-06-28","source":"CBS Sports / RotoWire","author":"RotoWire Staff","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/astros-cam-smith-nursing-sore-foot/"},{"note_id":"ne4bfda239c","article_date":"2026-10-16","source":"Astros On SI (Sports Illustrated)","author":"Thomas Heiberger","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/astros/onsi/can-astros-slugger-cam-smith-live-up-to-his-first-round-potential"},{"note_id":"n8c50d3cd0f","article_date":"2026-10-22","source":"Yahoo Sports / Houston Chronicle","author":"Matt Kawahara","url":"https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/fielder-cam-smith-progress-plate-120000718.html"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Cam Smith","team":"HOU","elig":"OF"},"p3488f87":{"text":"He broke Bob Hamelin's Royals rookie home-run record and was the American League Player of the Month and Rookie of the Month in September, when he hit .338 with eight homers and a 1.194 OPS. Kansas City took the hometown catcher in the third round in 2021, a risky high-school demographic, and after a slow climb he debuted in September 2025. In 2026 he hit .239/.323/.467 with 27 homers and 81 RBIs in 149 games, struck out 28 percent of the time, and finished fifth in AL Rookie of the Year voting; he was briefly benched on April 2 for sleeping through his alarm, and no injury from the season turned up in the notes. He started 97 games at catcher, graded plus-3 in framing and plus-2 in throwing, and Quatraro says he has stepped into the primary job, with the 2027 split with Salvy still to be determined by how both hold up to the workload.","sources":[{"note_id":"n2b83fef26e","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Carter_Jensen"},{"note_id":"n802ee2d00d","article_date":"2026-10-22","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-catchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nd3affa1365","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2026-mlb-rookie-of-the-year-voting-totals"},{"note_id":"ne7bfcf4cd2","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB Data Warehouse","author":"Jon Anderson","url":"https://www.mlbdatawarehouse.com/2027-team-previews-kansas-city-royals/"},{"note_id":"n74b5792a55","article_date":"2026-11-14","source":"MLB.com","author":"Perla Paredes","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/royals-plan-for-carter-jensen-salvador-perez-at-catcher-in-2027"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Carter Jensen","team":"KCR","elig":"C"},"p518ae45":{"text":"The White Sox took him 11th overall in 2017 out of Missouri State, and in 2018 he tore his left Achilles running out a ground ball, then tore it again walking in his backyard and needed a second surgery. Miami got him for Jake Eder, and Texas later acquired him for Echedry Vargas, Max Acosta and Brayan Mendoza; he wears No. 21 for his daughter Penelope. In 2026 he hit .228/.302/.421 with a team-high 28 homers and 90 RBIs, plus career highs in strikeouts (167) and walks (56), and a 95.1-mph sinker in August left him with a left-wrist contusion on the same wrist that had tendon-and-sheath surgery last offseason. The Rangers tendered him, and he is penciled in as the starting first baseman, though Matt Postins wouldn't be surprised if Justin Foscue pushes him in the spring, and there has been chatter that the Mets could have interest if Texas looks to move him.","sources":[{"note_id":"n84a0f37386","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Jake_Burger"},{"note_id":"na812e4981b","article_date":"2026-08-23","source":"MLB.com","author":"Kennedi Landry","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jake-burger-exits-vs-angels-after-being-hit-by-pitch"},{"note_id":"nee0f0ea99a","article_date":"2026-10-23","source":"Rangers On SI (Sports Illustrated)","author":"Matt Postins","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/rangers/onsi/projecting-rangers-2027-opening-day-lineup-how-different-could-it-get"},{"note_id":"n464cd33bd8","article_date":"2026-11-22","source":"Nolan Writin' (FanSided)","author":"Stephen Parello","url":"https://nolanwritin.com/jake-burger-survived-the-non-tender-deadline-but-that-doesn-t-secure-his-rangers-future-01m81djqmyrt"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Jake Burger","team":"TEX","elig":"1B/CI"},"p68739f":{"text":"He homered in Game 2 of the AL Wild Card Series, the 28th postseason home run of his career, one shy of Manny Ramirez's major-league record. Houston signed the 5-foot-6 second baseman out of Venezuela in 2006, and on Feb. 6, 2024, he agreed to a five-year, $125 million extension that runs through 2029. After a 2025 move to left field that Darragh McDonald calls a disaster, he returned to second full-time in 2026 and hit .250/.306/.395 with 16 homers, 50 RBIs and a 97 OPS+, his lowest in a full season since 2013, while defensive metrics were harsh: minus-17 runs saved and minus-10 outs above average. He has no 2026 injury in the notes and has been durable, but Yordan Alvarez occupies most of the DH at-bats, and with Paredes, Correa, Pena and Walker also in the infield mix, his 2027 position is again an open question.","sources":[{"note_id":"n06fc9cc9e1","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Jos%C3%A9_Altuve"},{"note_id":"n2f2c3925ff","article_date":"2026-02-28","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian McTaggart","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jose-altuve-hitting-adjustments-return-to-second-base"},{"note_id":"nc7ed66a423","article_date":"2026-10-03","source":"Yahoo Sports / Houston Chronicle","author":"Matt Kawahara","url":"https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/astros-offseason-questions-trade-jeremy-120000576.html"},{"note_id":"ncfb6465b37","article_date":"2026-10-05","source":"Bleacher Report","author":"Tim Kelly","url":"https://bleacherreport.com/articles/25506473-7-mlb-players-worse-you-think-2026"},{"note_id":"nad6222a054","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-second-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nc34d79fe39","article_date":"2026-11-03","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Darragh McDonald","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/11/offseason-outlook-houston-astros-17.html"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Jose Altuve","team":"HOU","elig":"2B/MI/OF"},"peaefc4d":{"text":"In 2024 he hit two three-homer games in the same season, something only Geronimo Berroa had done for the Athletics, and was the third-youngest player to do it, after Ralph Kiner and Mookie Betts. Oakland took him in the sixth round in 2018 out of a high school in Atlanta, nearly released him after a .177 season in 2019, and on March 6, 2025, signed him to a seven-year, $65.5 million extension. In 2026 he looked lost before the break, batting .196 with five homers, then hit .280 with 12 homers, 40 RBIs and an .853 OPS after it, finishing with 17 homers and 10 steals; his baserunning value sank from the 91st percentile in 2024 to the 4th percentile, after major knee surgery in October 2025. Andrew Ferguson wants the speed back in the middle of the lineup, and Jon Anderson says he will have to perform to stay on the field.","sources":[{"note_id":"n9a8704a9bc","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Lawrence_Butler"},{"note_id":"n9aa9c05acf","article_date":"2026-10-04","source":"Athletics On SI (Sports Illustrated)","author":"Andrew L. Ferguson Jr.","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/athletics/onsi/las-vegas-why-teams-will-fear-lawrence-butler-next-season"},{"note_id":"n4024269b35","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"MLB Data Warehouse","author":"Jon Anderson","url":"https://www.mlbdatawarehouse.com/2027-team-previews-athletics/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Lawrence Butler","team":"ATH","elig":"OF"},"p8cdb305":{"text":"In his first four games in 2022, he reached base 15 times, a stretch no player had matched since 1901, and he went 116 pitches before his first swing-and-miss. Cleveland took him in the fifth round in 2018 out of Oregon State's College World Series team, and he has since won four Gold Gloves in left and made two All-Star teams. In 2026 he had a .614 OPS over his first 354 plate appearances and an .875 OPS after the All-Star break, with a career-high 13.3 percent walk rate, and no injury from the season turned up in the notes; his last trip to the injured list was a 2024 hamstring strain. The Guardians tendered him for the final year of his contract, and Antonetti said he expects Kwan \"to be a big part of a contending team next year,\" even as trade chatter follows him.","sources":[{"note_id":"ncc531bed59","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Steven_Kwan"},{"note_id":"nb7212af1bc","article_date":"2026-11-08","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Mark Polishuk","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/11/looking-for-a-match-in-a-steven-kwan-trade.html"},{"note_id":"n30ef1410ad","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"Heavy","author":"Billy Heyen","url":"https://heavy.com/sports/mlb/cleveland-guardians/guardians-make-steven-kwan-roster-decision/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Steven Kwan","team":"CLE","elig":"OF"},"p930d6ea":{"text":"On July 18 he hit a go-ahead two-run homer, his second of the game, in Boston's 12th straight win, one of two straight two-homer games that made him the first Red Sox player to do that since Mookie Betts in 2016. Houston signed him out of Venezuela on July 2, 2017, and traded him to Boston on Aug. 1, 2022, in the Christian Vazquez deal. In 2026 he played 157 games, all but five of the club's schedule, hit .248/.327/.446 with an AL-leading 38 doubles and a career-high 24 homers, and won his third straight Gold Glove in right, leading all players with plus-28 defensive runs saved, the longest active streak by any player; he hit third in 106 of those games. He is under club control through 2030, and after a .207 September, Daniel Mader still sees him as a focal point of the 2027 lineup.","sources":[{"note_id":"n6b4681036c","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Wilyer_Abreu"},{"note_id":"n992e870c28","article_date":"2026-10-13","source":"NESN","author":"Daniel Mader","url":"https://nesn.com/boston-red-sox/news/red-sox-year-review-2026-wilyer-abreu-season/464479c663fa2899289d51f1"},{"note_id":"na1346ea37b","article_date":"2026-11-19","source":"MLB.com","author":"Theo DeRosa","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/red-sox-wilyer-abreu-among-most-underrated-players-mlb"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Wilyer Abreu","team":"BOS","elig":"OF"},"pe8bec9":{"text":"A year after he hit 60 home runs, the first 60-homer season by a catcher or a switch-hitter, and signed a six-year, $105 million extension, 2026 came apart: he was hitting .161 in 41 games when a right oblique strain, the first injured-list trip of his career, landed him on the shelf from May 14 to June 16. Seattle took him in the third round in 2018 out of Florida State; he still had the first three-homer game of his career on Aug. 24 and posted a .946 OPS with 11 homers over his final 36 games, but he finished at .182/.296/.368 with 23 homers, 69 RBIs and a .664 OPS in 126 games. On Oct. 3 he had a left-shoulder AC debridement and is expected to be fully recovered before spring training. GM Justin Hollander said he is \"really confident that we're going to get Cal Raleigh back as Cal Raleigh,\" even if that does not mean 60 homers again.","sources":[{"note_id":"neda1400f18","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Cal_Raleigh"},{"note_id":"ned5ce38bc1","article_date":"2026-09-29","source":"Seattle Sports (MyNorthwest)","author":"Brent Stecker","url":"https://sports.mynorthwest.com/mlb/seattle-mariners/seattle-mariners-justin-hollander-cal-raleigh-josh-naylor-julio-rodriguez"},{"note_id":"n6872aebbb6","article_date":"2026-10-05","source":"MLB.com (Mariners press release)","author":"Mariners Baseball Communications","url":"https://www.mlb.com/press-release/mariners-medical-update"},{"note_id":"n0408a43264","article_date":"2026-10-22","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-catchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-23","approved_by":"Steven","approved_date":"2026-11-23","name":"Cal Raleigh","team":"SEA","elig":"C"},"p98289fc":{"text":"He became only the fourth player in major league history to homer in his first three career games, and on Sept. 5 he became the first Japanese rookie to reach 30 homers. The 2022 Central League Triple Crown winner, who once homered in five straight at-bats for Yakult, came over when the White Sox, a dark horse in the posting, landed him on a two-year, $34 million deal. Through his first 60 games he hit .232/.371/.540 with 20 homers and was tied for the AL lead in homers when a Grade 2 right hamstring strain sent him to the IL at the end of May, and he faded badly late, hitting .156/.299/.375 over the final two months with a 35.4 percent strikeout rate. He still finished with 35 homers, a 130 OPS+ and fourth place in AL Rookie of the Year voting, and GM Chris Getz says extension talks have been going since late summer, with $17.5 million due in 2027.","sources":[{"note_id":"n424441d78b","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Munetaka_Murakami"},{"note_id":"n0af0cbd758","article_date":"2026-05-30","source":"MLB.com","author":"Scott Merkin","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/munetaka-murakami-to-injured-list-with-right-hamstring-strain"},{"note_id":"nb777fa48dd","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-first-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nd6f49ed8a9","article_date":"2026-10-14","source":"CBS Sports / RotoWire","author":"RotoWire Staff","url":"https://www.cbssports.com/fantasy/baseball/news/white-soxs-munetaka-murakami-discussing-contract-extension/"},{"note_id":"n7d467e4ea1","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2026-mlb-rookie-of-the-year-voting-totals"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Munetaka Murakami","team":"CHW","elig":"1B/CI"},"p7d103":{"text":"On July 30 he hit one of the rarest swings in baseball, an \"ultimate grand slam\": a walk-off slam with the Twins down three. Roger's son turned down Houston as a 35th-rounder to play at Texas, came back from Tommy John surgery to be Big 12 player of the year, and after stops in Detroit, which drafted him in the third round in 2018, and Philadelphia, he was sold to Minnesota in April 2025 in a roster crunch that became his break. In 2026 he set career highs nearly across the board, hitting .239 with 27 homers, 81 RBIs, 10 steals and a league-leading seven triples in 145 games, and won the Most Valuable Twin award. Jon Anderson of MLB Data Warehouse calls it empty power, pointing to a .254 xBA and exit velocities that don't support 25 to 30 homers, but after the Royce Lewis trade manager Derek Shelton leaned on Clemens' versatility, with Clemens able to play first or second around whoever the Twins add.","sources":[{"note_id":"n65907e9f24","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Kody_Clemens"},{"note_id":"n985377a02d","article_date":"2026-10-07","source":"Sports Illustrated (Twins On SI)","author":"Yirsandy Rodriguez","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/twins/onsi/twins-have-a-clear-decision-to-make-with-kody-clemens-after-his-breakout-2026-season"},{"note_id":"naee8fd0618","article_date":"2026-10-07","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/twins-2026-diamond-awards-winners"},{"note_id":"n25dd888459","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"MLB Data Warehouse","author":"Jon Anderson","url":"https://www.mlbdatawarehouse.com/2027-team-previews-minnesota-twins/"},{"note_id":"n812880d558","article_date":"2026-11-21","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/royce-lewis-traded-from-twins-to-dodgers"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Kody Clemens","team":"MIN","elig":"1B/2B/MI/CI/OF"},"pa4b9a9c":{"text":"He has zero career major league homers and was still one of nine AL outfield Silver Slugger finalists after hitting .306, fourth among qualifiers, with 46 steals in 151 games. An NCAA Division I batting champion at Georgia Tech, he signed with Tampa Bay for an under-slot $750,000 as the 70th pick in 2022, and his game is pure contact and speed: FanGraphs' Davy Andrews counted 362 \"competitive runs\" in 2026, the fourth-highest single-season total ever. He got better as the year went on, going from .279 with 22 steals before the break to .344 with 24 after it. He still sits against lefties (76 wRC+) and often batted fifth or sixth, and a foul ball off his right foot on Sept. 25 proved only a scare, and Adam Berry's early look at the 2027 Rays has him as the regular left fielder, where a full-time job atop the order would make him a steals, runs and average engine.","sources":[{"note_id":"nd749320e86","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jared Greenspan","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/chandler-simpson-named-2026-silver-slugger-award-outfield-finalist"},{"note_id":"nbd0154c879","article_date":"2022-07-30","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com (citing Jim Callis)","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/rays-2022-draft-signings-tracker"},{"note_id":"n73331679a6","article_date":"2026-10-20","source":"FanGraphs","author":"Davy Andrews","url":"https://blogs.fangraphs.com/chandler-simpson-could-be-the-king-of-competitive-runs/"},{"note_id":"n0158be472f","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Corbin Young","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/hitters-who-gained-15-of-value-in-the-second-half-of-2026/"},{"note_id":"n66327bd69f","article_date":"2026-10-02","source":"MLB.com","author":"Adam Berry","url":"https://www.mlb.com/rays/news/rays-get-tyler-wells-chandler-simpson-and-garrett-cleavinger-back-for-alds"},{"note_id":"n4d328333c5","article_date":"2026-11-24","source":"MLB.com","author":"Adam Berry","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/rays-potential-2027-26-man-roster-outlook"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Chandler Simpson","team":"TBR","elig":"OF"},"p8dd63cb":{"text":"The two-time World Series MVP hit .182/.292/.374 through 51 games before his second bout of lower back inflammation, then came back July 31 and hit .269 with 10 homers and an .804 OPS the rest of the way. The Dodgers took him 18th overall in 2012 for a $2.35 million bonus, he was the 2016 NL Rookie of the Year, and he is halfway through the 10-year, $325 million deal that brought him to Texas, with $155 million left over five seasons and full no-trade protection. Health is the story: he has averaged 112 games over the past four seasons, played 102 in each of the last two, and had a bone spur removed from his right elbow on Oct. 6, so he will enter a season coming off surgery for the third straight year, though Texas expects him ready for normal offseason work. His .736 OPS and 23.6 percent strikeout rate were career worsts, but a .254 BABIP hints at bad luck, and while the Rangers are open to moving him, ESPN's Jeff Passan reports Seager has a narrow list of teams he'd accept and there's no momentum toward a deal.","sources":[{"note_id":"n5821dbfc5d","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Corey_Seager"},{"note_id":"ne91457b145","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"Dallas Sports Journal","author":"Grant Afseth","url":"https://dallassportsjournal.com/corey-seager-injury-history-elbow-surgery-trade/"},{"note_id":"nb400c97f08","article_date":"2026-11-11","source":"MLB.com","author":"Kennedi Landry","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/rangers-could-consider-trading-corey-seager-other-stars"},{"note_id":"n975054e604","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Darragh McDonald","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/11/latest-on-corey-seager-2.html"},{"note_id":"n2ab9396b95","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Corey Seager","team":"TEX","elig":"SS/MI"},"pb8dee08":{"text":"Texas signed him out of the Bahamas for $3.2 million in 2023, and his two loudest tools are double-plus raw power and a cannon arm; he had the Arizona Fall League's best infield arm this fall. Arm fatigue in last year's Fall League turned into internal brace surgery on his throwing elbow in February, costing him the first three and a half months of 2026 and a likely starting job. Once he could DH he raked, hitting .325/.401/.590 with 10 homers in 41 games, mostly at Double-A, then went back to shortstop in the AFL and hit .303/.351/.455 with some of the league's best exit velocities, though just one homer in 16 games. The knocks are some swing-and-miss and not enough lift, and ZiPS doesn't see a 2027 impact, but MLB Pipeline picked him as the Rangers' 2027 Rookie of the Year candidate and Pitcher List's John Villavicencio sees a realistic chance at Texas at-bats as early as Opening Day.","sources":[{"note_id":"n50250a5302","article_date":"2026-10-26","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/sebastian-walcott-2027-fantasy-outlook/"},{"note_id":"na4fe483ec3","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/top-30-arizona-fall-league-prospects-2026"},{"note_id":"n53c8db3f14","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"},{"note_id":"n26b12f24b3","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"Pitcher List","author":"John Villavicencio","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/afl-dynasty-takeaways-2026/"},{"note_id":"neadd6f5bfc","article_date":"2026-11-16","source":"FanGraphs","author":"Dan Szymborski","url":"https://blogs.fangraphs.com/2027-zips-projections-texas-rangers/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Sebastian Walcott","team":"TEX","elig":"SS/MI"},"p243d357":{"text":"On Aug. 24, 2022, he opened a game against Washington with 24 straight strikes, the most by any pitcher since pitch tracking began in 1988. Seattle made the Elon right-hander the 20th overall pick in 2019, the highest-drafted player in school history, after he passed on the Mets as a 32nd-rounder in 2016. Right shoulder inflammation cost him nearly three months at the start of 2025, and while he stayed healthy in 2026 and led the AL in complete games, he finished with a career-worst 4.33 ERA (4.20 expected) as he threw seven different pitches at least 80 times, abandoned his splitter midseason and watched opponents hit .251 off a four-seamer that averaged a career-best 96.7 mph. The velocity is still there, and Jerry Dipoto says Seattle is done dealing from its rotation, with Kirby in an Opening Day group alongside Logan Gilbert, Bryan Woo, Emerson Hancock and Kade Anderson.","sources":[{"note_id":"nee127d7274","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/George_Kirby"},{"note_id":"na0314cc154","article_date":"2025-05-22","source":"MLB.com","author":"Ryan Herrera; Daniel Kramer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/george-kirby-set-for-2025-debut-vs-astros"},{"note_id":"ne32341d399","article_date":"2026-10-06","source":"Sports Illustrated (Mariners On SI)","author":"Tremayne Person","url":"https://www.si.com/mlb/mariners/onsi/mariners-vaunted-pitching-lab-may-have-overengineered-george-kirby"},{"note_id":"n28dcfcaed7","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"MLB.com","author":"Daniel Kramer","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mariners-plan-for-2026-27-offseason-after-maikel-garcia-trade"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"George Kirby","team":"SEA","elig":"SP"},"pae3a2b7":{"text":"He made his third straight All-Star team in 2026 and posted the best slash line of his career, hitting .280 with an .844 OPS while cutting his strikeout rate to a career-best 26.5 percent. Detroit took him fifth overall in 2019 and signed him for the full $6,180,700 slot before the draft was even over, pulling him away from a Florida commitment. The power dipped to 21 homers and a right hamstring strain cost him three weeks in August, but Jon Anderson of MLB Data Warehouse notes his 14.8 percent barrel rate was still elite and blames too many fly balls to center rather than lost juice. The Tigers tendered him on Nov. 20, and while industry executives have floated him as a trade candidate, Scott Harris says he wants Greene \"a big part of the middle of our order,\" with free agency not until after 2028.","sources":[{"note_id":"nb93d853c5a","article_date":"2019-06-05","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jason Beck","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/riley-greene-signed-by-tigers"},{"note_id":"n9aa670d616","article_date":"2026-09-05","source":"MLB.com","author":"Mark Feinsand","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/tigers-offseason-needs-free-agent-targets-2026-27"},{"note_id":"n9418522d75","article_date":"2026-09-02","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jason Beck; Patrick Donnelly","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/riley-greene-tigers-injury-return-2026"},{"note_id":"n87dbc28ff7","article_date":"2026-11-11","source":"MLB Data Warehouse","author":"Jon Anderson","url":"https://www.mlbdatawarehouse.com/2027-team-previews-detroit-tigers/"},{"note_id":"nc4720c127d","article_date":"2026-11-23","source":"Yahoo Sports (Detroit Sports Nation)","author":"Richard Knight","url":"https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/riley-greene-trade-rumors-could-233000211.html"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Riley Greene","team":"DET","elig":"OF"},"pfd56a92":{"text":"The three-time AL MVP made his 12th All-Star team in 2026 and hit 22 homers with 11 steals, his first double-digit steal season since 2019, as baseball's oldest regular center fielder. The Angels took him 25th overall in 2009 for a $1,215,000 bonus, signed by scout Greg Morhardt, a former minor league teammate of his father, Jeff. He played 74 of the Angels' first 75 games and led the AL with 54 runs before a right hamstring strain in June, and he has now played 272 games over two seasons after years lost to calf, back, hamate and knee injuries. Jon Anderson of MLB Data Warehouse notes he had by far the fewest homers among 600-plate-appearance hitters with a 15 percent barrel rate, so more power could come, and with Kroenke buying the club, Trout, 35 with four years left on his deal, says he wants to stay and win, with a move to left field expected.","sources":[{"note_id":"nd3a975867e","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Mike_Trout"},{"note_id":"na1b4e1610c","article_date":"2026-06-19","source":"MLB.com","author":"Rhett Bollinger","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/mike-trout-injured-list-with-hamstring-injury"},{"note_id":"n03ddfdfeb2","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"MLB Data Warehouse","author":"Jon Anderson","url":"https://www.mlbdatawarehouse.com/2027-team-previews-los-angeles-angels/"},{"note_id":"n9b5c7c465f","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"TSN (ESPN)","author":"Alden Gonzalez","url":"https://www.tsn.ca/mlb/article/why-trout-believes-new-owner-kroenke-will-revive-angels-n1-50531850/"},{"note_id":"n0284f4eba5","article_date":"2026-11-07","source":"MLB.com","author":"Rhett Bollinger","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/where-angels-roster-stands-entering-offseason"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Mike Trout","team":"LAA","elig":"OF"},"pd3ac94a":{"text":"His 18 homers were the second-most ever by an AL or NL rookie catcher 21 or younger, and he didn't turn 22 until August, spending most of his first full season against big league pitching instead of the minors. The line was modest at .222/.291/.406 with 54 RBIs, a 26.6 percent strikeout rate and a 12.1 percent barrel rate, and lefties gave him real trouble, with a 30 wRC+ against them versus 116 against righties, so projections have him sitting some against southpaws. He also played 12 games at first base, and with Adley Rutschman gone, Vlad Sedler of RotoGraphs calls him the Orioles' catcher of the present and the future.","sources":[{"note_id":"ndc571416dc","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/ranking-2026-rookies-based-on-long-term-value"},{"note_id":"n8d638a2896","article_date":"2026-10-22","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-catchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n92cf0b6b51","article_date":"2026-10-29","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-first-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n30a8047e83","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Vlad Sedler","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/early-adp-observations-catchers/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Samuel Basallo","team":"BAL","elig":"C/1B/CI"},"p9d1de2a":{"text":"Houston took the Maine shortstop, the son of former big leaguer Geronimo Peña, in the third round in 2018 for a $535,000 bonus after rating him the best defensive shortstop in the draft. Through late August, Houston was 43-33 when he started and 21-31 when he didn't, and Joe Espada calls him a sparkplug who makes the lineup longer on both sides of the ball. His 2026 was a string of injuries: a fractured fingertip knocked him out of the World Baseball Classic in March, a right hamstring strain cost him more than a month in April and May, a left calf strain took 10 days in July, and a 100 mph pitch off his right hand sidelined him again in August. He still hit .277/.325/.477 with 22 homers, 56 RBIs and 12 steals in 112 games, with 16 of those homers coming after the break compared with six before it. Coming off a 2025 in which he hit .304 with 17 homers and 20 steals, the power surge is the reason for optimism if he can stay on the field.","sources":[{"note_id":"n85cc46c972","article_date":"2018-06-05","source":"MLB.com","author":"Christian Boutwell","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/astros-tab-position-players-on-day-2-of-draft-c279918374"},{"note_id":"n20ad5f7a00","article_date":"","source":"Baseball Almanac","author":"Baseball Almanac","url":"https://www.baseball-almanac.com/players/player.php?p=penaje02"},{"note_id":"nc7b7c5971b","article_date":"2026-07-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian McTaggart","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jeremy-pena-off-injured-list-returns-to-astros-after-left-calf-strain"},{"note_id":"ndfb0e71e67","article_date":"2026-08-21","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian McTaggart","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jeremy-pena-exits-with-right-hand-contusion-vs-angels"},{"note_id":"nb90e7e0153","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n58a4709ade","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Corbin Young","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/hitters-who-gained-15-of-value-in-the-second-half-of-2026/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Jeremy Pena","team":"HOU","elig":"SS/MI"},"pe8c1474":{"text":"He doesn't turn 23 until December and flew through the Twins' system to debut this summer, playing all 33 of his big league games at shortstop and hitting .274/.328/.393 with three homers. In a half season at Triple-A he hit .271 with 14 homers, 17 steals, an 11.4 percent walk rate and an 18.7 percent strikeout rate in 295 at-bats, and he stole 25 bases in the 2025 minor league season. Jacob Milham of Pitcher List cautions that a .287 xwOBA and 3.4 percent barrel rate trailed the results, and late-season shoulder and hamstring injuries cut into his debut. He can also play second and third, he is projected as Minnesota's 2027 starting shortstop, and Razzball's Grey likes the power, average and enough sprint speed for 15-plus steals.","sources":[{"note_id":"n508b865f77","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n461a0dd2f5","article_date":"2026-11-15","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/kaelen-culpepper-2027-fantasy-outlook/"},{"note_id":"nd81b90b116","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"Pitcher List","author":"Jacob Milham","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/dynasty-value-of-2026-late-season-hitter-promotions/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Kaelen Culpepper","team":"MIN","elig":"SS/MI"},"p6377140":{"text":"Before his 26-game big league debut at 21, he hit .323/.417/.559 in 73 minor league games, with a 13.4 percent walk rate, a 15.4 percent strikeout rate and 17 steals at Triple-A. In Minnesota he hit just .226 with four homers and weak exit velocity, but he held his strikeout rate at 20.4 percent with a 7.5 percent walk rate. Health is the catch: he has never topped 100 games in a season, battled injuries in 2024 and 2025, and had a bone chip and spur removed from his right elbow around Oct. 1, though the Twins say he'll be a full go for 2027. MLB Pipeline picked him as the Twins' 2027 Rookie of the Year candidate and expects him in the lineup from Opening Day, and having already been promoted, there's little reason to hold him down.","sources":[{"note_id":"n29c76fd02b","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"},{"note_id":"n36a62f38be","article_date":"2026-11-01","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/walker-jenkins-2027-fantasy-outlook/"},{"note_id":"n0f5c66d0e6","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"Pitcher List","author":"Jacob Milham","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/dynasty-value-of-2026-late-season-hitter-promotions/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Walker Jenkins","team":"MIN","elig":"OF"},"pdc823df":{"text":"The Eastern League MVP reached Triple-A at 20 and hit .323/.400/.557 with 25 homers and nine steals in 117 games across Double-A and Triple-A, striking out just 14.2 percent of the time. MLB Pipeline, which picked him as Boston's 2027 Rookie of the Year candidate, calls him a better option than Trevor Story right now on both sides of the ball, and Razzball's The Itch was surprised he didn't debut in September. Not everyone is sold: a Red Sox scout told Razzball's Grey, \"He won't translate one to one and he's very slow,\" and wasn't sure he would break camp. Grey's comp is Zach Neto with more average, and he wonders whether Boston rolls Story out until July before turning short over to Arias.","sources":[{"note_id":"n79c732d321","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"},{"note_id":"n6e8fccd92b","article_date":"2026-11-15","source":"Razzball","author":"The Itch","url":"https://razzball.com/boston-red-sox-top-10-prospects-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n1a3fbd0042","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/franklin-arias-2027-fantasy-outlook/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Franklin Arias","team":"BOS","elig":"SS/MI"},"p5d22ea3":{"text":"He led the minors with 69 extra-base hits and hit 37 homers at 20, the first White Sox prospect to reach 30 since Luis Robert Jr. in 2019, slashing .251/.380/.550 with 17 steals across High-A and Double-A. Contact was the worry after a 29.1 percent strikeout rate in 61 High-A games, but after the jump to Double-A Birmingham he hit 19 more homers in 71 games, cut the strikeouts to 25.2 percent and lifted his zone-contact rate to 87.5 percent. He kept it going in the Arizona Fall League, hitting .286/.364/.633 with three homers in 13 games and bat speed, exit velocity and squared-up rates all in the 95th percentile, and he played center and left to widen his path. GM Chris Getz says he will open 2027 at Triple-A, where a hot first half could bring a July call-up.","sources":[{"note_id":"ne79909ecd7","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"},{"note_id":"ndfc1965f67","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"Pitcher List","author":"Jacob Milham","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/dynasty-time-machine-predicting-the-top-10-prospects-one-year-from-now/"},{"note_id":"n3d26f8fe19","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/top-30-arizona-fall-league-prospects-2026"},{"note_id":"n194f953b17","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"Pitcher List","author":"John Villavicencio","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/afl-dynasty-takeaways-2026/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Caleb Bonemer","team":"CHW","elig":"3B/SS/MI/CI"},"pe7e8f9f":{"text":"His 239 strikeouts in 169 innings were the fourth-most in Blue Jays history, part of an 11-6, 2.40 ERA, 1.101 WHIP bounce-back from a 2025 in which a 3.56 FIP said his 4.55 ERA was unlucky. Toronto signed him last December to a seven-year, $210 million deal, the biggest free-agent contract in franchise history, and year one already looks better than the day he signed. The catch is the shoulder: he first felt it after a July 25 complete game in Boston, his fastball slid from near 100 mph to the mid-90s and lower, and he finished the season on the IL with right shoulder inflammation. The MRI showed nothing structural, the Blue Jays don't expect it to spill into 2027, and Cease says rest and shoulder work should handle it, so spring velocity is the thing to watch.","sources":[{"note_id":"ndbd5385baa","article_date":"2026-10-15","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-100-dynasty-starting-pitchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nafec7004f0","article_date":"2025-12-02","source":"MLB.com","author":"Keegan Matheson","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/dylan-cease-blue-jays-deal"},{"note_id":"n9e13d54621","article_date":"2026-09-23","source":"MLB.com","author":"Keegan Matheson","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/dylan-cease-right-shoulder-inflammation-mri"},{"note_id":"nef4647d8fb","article_date":"2026-10-18","source":"Blue Jays Insider","author":"Victor William","url":"https://www.bluejaysinsider.com/mlb-team/toronto-blue-jays/dylan-cease-speaks-on-his-injury-setback-and-plan-moving-forward"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Dylan Cease","team":"TOR","elig":"SP"},"p27e9e06":{"text":"He won the AL Gold Glove in left field, leading all big league left fielders with plus-21 Defensive Runs Saved, and he was this year's All-Star Game MVP. The Yankees brought him back in January on a five-year deal reported at $162.5 million, with a $20 million signing bonus, a full no-trade clause and opt-outs after 2027 and 2028. A Grade 2 left hamstring strain cost him about a month starting July 25, and his power slipped from 29 homers to 17, with a .264/.338/.429 line, 73 runs, 73 RBIs and 12 steals in 132 games. His bat speed sits in the 21st percentile, but an 88th-percentile sweet-spot rate suggests the offense can bounce back, and the Yankees have no plans to move him out of left.","sources":[{"note_id":"n8cc25e64be","article_date":"2026-01-26","source":"MLB.com","author":"Bryan Hoch","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/cody-bellinger-yankees-deal"},{"note_id":"n3eda4c1d9f","article_date":"2026-08-23","source":"MLB.com","author":"Bryan Hoch","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/cody-bellinger-yankees-hamstring-injury-rehab"},{"note_id":"n895b4f5ac1","article_date":"2026-10-30","source":"Yahoo Sports (SB Nation)","author":"Andrés Chávez","url":"https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/yankees-2026-roster-report-cards-133000914.html"},{"note_id":"n4a5a44d377","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"Yanks Go Yard (FanSided)","author":"Adam Weinrib","url":"https://yanksgoyard.com/cody-bellinger-gold-glove-nod-is-yet-another-reminder-yankees-position-switch-isnt-coming"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Cody Bellinger","team":"NYY","elig":"1B/CI/OF"},"p76b3e3":{"text":"He robbed three home runs from Seattle in a 1-0 win on April 4, and he still has elite bat speed at 77.3 mph, 97th percentile in the majors. The Angels drafted him 10th overall in 2017 with a $4.3 million bonus, and a year after his 37-homer, 98-RBI 2025, the last-place Angels traded him to Cleveland on Aug. 3 for catching prospect Jacob Cozart while he was hitting .239 with 16 homers. As a Guardian he crushed lefties (.358/.443/.660, 206 wRC+) but managed only .197/.273/.315 against righties, in line with a career 81 wRC+ against them, and Cleveland chose a hobbled Chase DeLauter over him in right field in the playoffs. The Guardians tendered him for his final year of club control, but Covering the Corner's Quincy Wheeler warns that counting on him as a full-time hitter would be a mistake, which points to a platoon role.","sources":[{"note_id":"n63d811041b","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Jo_Adell"},{"note_id":"nc1764568ba","article_date":"2026-08-04","source":"MLB.com","author":"Tim Stebbins","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jo-adell-traded-to-guardians-from-angels"},{"note_id":"nbff4451986","article_date":"2026-11-19","source":"Covering the Corner (SB Nation)","author":"Quincy Wheeler","url":"https://www.coveringthecorner.com/cleveland-guardians-analysis/76809/guardians-season-in-review-jo-adell"},{"note_id":"n9498ab1daa","article_date":"2026-11-21","source":"MLB.com","author":"Tim Stebbins","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/guardians-non-tender-deadline-decisions-2026"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Jo Adell","team":"CLE","elig":"OF"},"pbd0561e":{"text":"He finished tied for ninth in AL Rookie of the Year voting after a debut season of 15 homers, 57 RBIs and 19 steals with a .245/.335/.408 line. He opened with an outstanding May (.311/.398/.476 with eight steals), slumped from June through August, then righted himself with a .280/.365/.613 final month that included six homers. That late surge is why Razzball's Jakkers sees all the signs of a step forward in year two.","sources":[{"note_id":"n992faa35d9","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com","author":"MLB.com","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/2026-mlb-rookie-of-the-year-voting-totals"},{"note_id":"n77a129f257","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-second-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Travis Bazzana","team":"CLE","elig":"2B/MI"},"p4bc46e0":{"text":"The Angels traded Logan O'Hoppe away and acquired him from the Cubs fully intending to make him their starting catcher, which leaves him a wide-open path to a full-time job. It hasn't clicked yet: he hit .231/.303/.385 with Chicago and only .199/.260/.280 after the trade. Vlad Sedler of RotoGraphs asks whether his Angels role will be more than strong-side platoon DH, and whether the rough rookie year has left him undervalued.","sources":[{"note_id":"n6b10b6de50","article_date":"2026-10-22","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-catchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n6256d47602","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Vlad Sedler","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/early-adp-observations-catchers/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Moises Ballesteros","team":"LAA","elig":"C/1B/CI"},"p0a2b0d2":{"text":"Baltimore took him 19th overall in 2025 and announced him as a catcher, but he spent 2026 in the outfield and at first base and thrived, hitting .286/.388/.528 with 16 homers and 20 steals in 76 games. He kept it going in the Arizona Fall League at .328/.439/.507 with five steals in 18 games, and Jim Callis says the left-handed hitter controls the strike zone and hits the ball hard to all fields. He is still learning the outfield after catching for much of his amateur career, but Callis expects him to be a capable defender in right, and Razzball's The Itch says he could move quickly enough to debut in 2027.","sources":[{"note_id":"nca89dfa7bd","article_date":"2026-11-11","source":"Razzball","author":"The Itch","url":"https://razzball.com/baltimore-orioles-top-10-prospects-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"neda458b036","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/top-30-arizona-fall-league-prospects-2026"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Ike Irish","team":"BAL","elig":"1B/CI/OF"},"p3f9d52f":{"text":"He hit .371/.439/.514 with a homer and two steals in a 15-game look with Seattle, nine of them at second base, after hitting .283/.359/.471 with 18 homers, 81 RBIs and 17 steals between Double-A and Triple-A. Pitcher List's Jacob Milham says he had the best approach of the late-season call-ups he studied, with a 14.6 percent strikeout rate, a 12.2 percent walk rate and a .375 xwOBA, though a .414 BABIP propped up the average. Pitcher List's Martin Sekulski calls the power misleading, noting his best seasons came in hitter-friendly Modesto and Everett, and calls his defense mediocre. The bigger problem is the path: Gold Glove winner Cole Young has second base, so left field looks like his likeliest route to at-bats, which puts his second-base eligibility at risk.","sources":[{"note_id":"n3cc1195241","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-second-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n55d43437f8","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"Pitcher List","author":"Jacob Milham","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/dynasty-value-of-2026-late-season-hitter-promotions/"},{"note_id":"nee7f201c8d","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"Pitcher List","author":"Martin Sekulski","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/projection-based-prospect-rankings-second-base/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Michael Arroyo","team":"SEA","elig":"2B/MI"},"pf12b4da":{"text":"He hit .290/.379/.548 with 29 homers in 117 games across Double-A and Triple-A at 21, with a 19.9 percent strikeout rate and an 11.3 percent walk rate, and his highlight reel includes a three-homer game and 13 homers in a 14-game stretch. Razzball's Grey calls the lefty-slugging first baseman, whose given name is Raffaele, a Jim Thome type and about as surefire as bats come. There is no speed to speak of, just two steals all year, so the value is all in the bat, and MLB Pipeline picked him as Cleveland's 2027 Rookie of the Year candidate, expecting him to add average and power. Grey reads Kyle Manzardo's hold on first base as written in pencil.","sources":[{"note_id":"nbd79815c9b","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"},{"note_id":"n823a812080","article_date":"2026-11-16","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/ralphy-velazquez-2027-fantasy-outlook/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Ralphy Velazquez","team":"CLE","elig":"1B/CI/OF"},"p64ab4f3":{"text":"He was the key piece Detroit got back from the Dodgers in the Tarik Skubal trade, and he made his Tigers debut on Sept. 21 with three scoreless innings, touching 98.1 mph. He had a 1.33 ERA in 20 1/3 innings for the Dodgers in 2024 before Tommy John surgery cost him all of 2025, and injuries have limited him to 69 1/3 innings over three years. The arsenal runs deep, with a 97 mph fastball, 94 mph cutter, 91 mph slider, sinker, curve and changeup, the curve and fastball the standouts, and his walk rate was a tidy 2 per nine this year. He is 28, finished September healthy after two starts and 4 1/3 innings with Detroit, and MLB Pipeline, which picked him as the Tigers' 2027 Rookie of the Year candidate, sees a rotation chance out of the gate.","sources":[{"note_id":"nca840f24a8","article_date":"2026-10-15","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-100-dynasty-starting-pitchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n2c73ec43d3","article_date":"2026-11-19","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/river-ryan-2027-fantasy-outlook/"},{"note_id":"n20d658cd8a","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"River Ryan","team":"DET","elig":"SP"},"p2085963":{"text":"He is entering the third year of the eight-year, $218 million deal the Yankees gave him, the largest ever for a left-handed pitcher, after a 2026 interrupted twice by a bone bruise in his pitching elbow, in May and again in August. He was still effective when he pitched, going 5-5 with a 2.80 ERA over 20 starts and 112 2/3 innings, a year after leading the AL in wins and winning percentage at 19-5 with a 2.86 ERA. San Diego drafted him seventh overall out of Harvard-Westlake in 2012, the first high school pitcher taken, and he headlined the package Atlanta got for Justin Upton, later throwing six scoreless innings in the 2021 World Series clincher. He had a bone spur removed from the elbow this fall, and Brian Cashman says he will be ready for spring training, with his throwing program starting the second week of December.","sources":[{"note_id":"n0ed3bdd6f7","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Max_Fried"},{"note_id":"n76b1575287","article_date":"2026-11-11","source":"MLB.com","author":"Bryan Hoch","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/max-fried-elbow-surgery-to-be-ready-for-spring-training"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Max Fried","team":"NYY","elig":"SP"},"p48b4a89":{"text":"Kansas City signed him for $2.9 million in January, and he immediately became one of the best hitters in the Dominican Summer League, batting .361/.467/.653 with nine homers, 15 steals and more walks (28) than strikeouts (19) in 47 games while playing strong defense in center. The Royals then sent him straight to the Arizona Fall League, where at 17 years and 291 days he was the second-youngest player in league history, and he held his own with a .311/.415/.444 line and a 47 percent hard-hit rate. Jim Callis sees a future plus hitter with power to match, though reports on his speed range from solid to well above average. This is a long-horizon stash rather than 2027 help, and the Royals haven't decided whether he opens 2027 at Low-A.","sources":[{"note_id":"nb6adb90976","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"Pitcher List","author":"Jacob Milham","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/dynasty-time-machine-predicting-the-top-10-prospects-one-year-from-now/"},{"note_id":"n2f42e3b5e1","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"Pitcher List","author":"John Villavicencio","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/afl-dynasty-takeaways-2026/"},{"note_id":"n6bbe5288c2","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/top-30-arizona-fall-league-prospects-2026"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Angeibel Gomez","team":"KCR","elig":"OF"},"p4cdd205":{"text":"He hit a 497-foot grand slam at Worcester in June 2025, longer than any homer in the majors that season, and finished third in AL Rookie of the Year voting after hitting .292 with a .396 OBP in 71 games. Boston drafted him in the second round out of high school in 2022 and signed him in August 2025 to an eight-year, $130 million extension with escalators that could add another $100 million. His 2026 was derailed by a partially torn tendon connecting his right ring finger to his wrist, which kept him out until the next-to-last day of August, and after four homers and a .733 OPS in 23 games he aggravated it, with new right wrist strains, in Game 1 of the Wild Card Series. The underlying skills held, with a 12 percent walk rate, a 46.6 percent hard-hit rate and an 11.7 percent barrel rate, and Craig Breslow hopes he will be ready to go at spring training, with rest rather than surgery the plan. NBC Sports Boston's Justin Leger projects him as Boston's Opening Day left fielder, batting leadoff.","sources":[{"note_id":"n2d0e6b2d19","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Roman_Anthony"},{"note_id":"n89c1984967","article_date":"2026-11-03","source":"MLB.com","author":"Brian Murphy","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/roman-anthony-analysis-for-red-sox-2027-season"},{"note_id":"n42b96a039b","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"Boston.com","author":"Hayden Bird","url":"https://www.boston.com/sports/morning-sports-update/2026/11/10/what-craig-breslow-had-to-say-about-roman-anthony-triston-casas-other-red-sox-injuries/"},{"note_id":"nc0dea41fa0","article_date":"2026-11-19","source":"NBC Sports Boston","author":"Justin Leger","url":"https://www.nbcboston.com/mlb/boston-red-sox/roster-reset-offseason-depth-chart-2027/4051654/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Roman Anthony","team":"BOS","elig":"OF"},"pbd7c94a":{"text":"He was the NLCS MVP as Milwaukee swept the Dodgers, working a 12-pitch walk to set up a Game 2 win and capping an 11-pitch at-bat with a go-ahead homer off Blake Snell in Game 3. A seventh-round Padres pick in 2013 who was traded to Tampa Bay in the Wil Myers deal and later to Cleveland for Yandy Diaz, he finally broke through in 2026, taking first base from Andrew Vaughn and hitting .266 with 26 homers, 86 RBIs and a 140 OPS+ in 144 games. Boston signed him this month to a four-year, $58 million deal, a bit of a surprise since Willson Contreras is the unquestioned starter at first and Masataka Yoshida is penciled in at DH. His at-bats will come at DH and on the outfield corners, where he played 16 games in left and 32 in right for Milwaukee, and Craig Breslow and Chad Tracy have all winter to work out the fit.","sources":[{"note_id":"nf203882092","article_date":"2026-11-17","source":"MLB.com","author":"Ian Browne","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/jake-bauers-4-year-contract-with-red-sox"},{"note_id":"n1ed9fd78be","article_date":"","source":"BR Bullpen (Baseball-Reference)","author":"BR Bullpen contributors","url":"https://www.baseball-reference.com/bullpen/Jake_Bauers"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Jake Bauers","team":"BOS","elig":"1B/CI"},"p218e0b7":{"text":"He spent all of 2026 at Double-A Midland as a 19-year-old, a Texas League All-Star and Futures Game pick after hitting .426 in big league camp, and finished at .290/.378/.458 with 13 homers and 34 steals. The switch-hitter has real power, real speed and a steady approach, though the power breakout is still waiting, with his ground-ball rate climbing to 42.6 percent. His season ended Aug. 14 and he had surgery to repair the labrum in his right throwing shoulder, with a recovery of up to eight months, though a 2026 study found 92.9 percent of position players who had the repair came back without a significant drop. Jacob Milham of Pitcher List points to a return around May and a late-summer debut, possibly at second base, since the A's have locked up Jacob Wilson at shortstop.","sources":[{"note_id":"n34ce973044","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"},{"note_id":"n483fc65fe5","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"Pitcher List","author":"Jacob Milham","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/dynasty-time-machine-predicting-the-top-10-prospects-one-year-from-now/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Leo De Vries","team":"ATH","elig":"SS/MI"},"pda33ea0":{"text":"The 6-foot-7, 248-pound right-hander throws 102 mph and struck out Aaron Judge with a 103 mph fastball in live batting practice this spring, and the Yankees signed him for just $10,000 in February 2022. Command is the hurdle: he moved to the Triple-A bullpen in early June after struggling to find the zone, finished with a 4.48 ERA and 85 strikeouts in 64 1/3 innings, and hurt his right shoulder in July. He came back for one clean inning in late September, striking out two without a walk. The Yankees still talk about him as a starter, but MLB Pipeline sees the bullpen as his likelier 2027 role.","sources":[{"note_id":"n4903e5351e","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"},{"note_id":"n5c95abce3c","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"Razzball","author":"The Itch","url":"https://razzball.com/new-york-yankees-top-10-prospects-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Carlos Lagrange","team":"NYY","elig":"RP"},"pe7b3e25":{"text":"At 6-foot-8 and 200 pounds, the left-hander has the build of an NBA wing and surprisingly sharp command, with a low three-quarters release that is a nightmare for left-handed hitters. In 131 innings, mostly at Double-A, he posted a 2.27 ERA and 0.90 WHIP with 149 strikeouts against just 30 walks, and he is throwing his breaking stuff with more confidence and regularity. MLB Pipeline calls him the most advanced of Baltimore's Double-A arms and picked him as the Orioles' 2027 Rookie of the Year candidate, with a chance at an early shot in the big league rotation.","sources":[{"note_id":"n5f438cb91a","article_date":"2026-11-11","source":"Razzball","author":"The Itch","url":"https://razzball.com/baltimore-orioles-top-10-prospects-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n2bfc419908","article_date":"2026-11-10","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jonathan Mayo, Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/each-team-2027-rookie-of-the-year-candidate"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Joseph Dzierwa","team":"BAL","elig":"SP"},"pf92e233":{"text":"Razzball's Grey calls him \"Big Blaster,\" and the power is real: a 17.9 percent barrel rate and a 474-foot homer, on top of 38 homers and eight steals in the minors at 21. The problem is contact, with a 40.7 percent strikeout rate and a 44.6 percent whiff rate after his call-up, the worst in Jacob Milham's group of late-season promotions, after a 30.6 percent strikeout rate in the minors. Grey thinks he can live with strikeouts near 30 percent but not 40, and worries he could hit .175. Seattle hasn't decided between a regular role and Triple-A, though Grey says he seems to be in the Mariners' immediate plans.","sources":[{"note_id":"n13dbdbe05c","article_date":"2026-11-08","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/lazaro-montes-2027-fantasy-outlook/"},{"note_id":"n05bf88ccca","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"Pitcher List","author":"Jacob Milham","url":"https://pitcherlist.com/dynasty-value-of-2026-late-season-hitter-promotions/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Lazaro Montes","team":"SEA","elig":"OF"},"p30f9705":{"text":"He was the youngest player in the American League in 2026 and the fifth-youngest Mariner ever to debut, splitting time between shortstop and third base. The 69-game debut was rough, a .190/.246/.335 line, though he still hit eight homers with 23 RBIs. He is still just 21, and Razzball's Jakkers sees the tools for a 20-to-25-homer player with about 10 steals once the bat catches up.","sources":[{"note_id":"n2a6018324c","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/ranking-2026-rookies-based-on-long-term-value"},{"note_id":"n64ce70ba79","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Colt Emerson","team":"SEA","elig":"3B/SS/MI/CI"},"p19f9919":{"text":"He looked like a breakout star as a 2023 rookie, smashing 23 homers with a .282/.308/.538 line in 104 games, and followed with solid seasons of 16 homers and 84 RBIs in 2024 and 20 and 70 in 2025. His 2026 slipped to 14 homers and 45 RBIs in 98 games as he shared time with Christian Vazquez and lost about a month to an oblique strain. The finish was strong, though: after a slow start he hit .318 with eight homers and 22 RBIs over 50 second-half games, which is why Vlad Sedler of RotoGraphs sees a bounce-back with a power-and-average baseline.","sources":[{"note_id":"ne1b5268baf","article_date":"2026-10-22","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-catchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nadb4b16328","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Vlad Sedler","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/early-adp-observations-catchers/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Yainer Diaz","team":"HOU","elig":"C"},"p119a72d":{"text":"He jumped from five homers and a .649 OPS in 2025 to .276/.350/.415 with 16 homers, 31 doubles, 63 RBIs and 90 runs in 2026. The catch is a massive platoon split: .350/.431/.592 against lefties but .247/.318/.347 against righties. His steals also fell from 14 to four, so Razzball's Jakkers says the next step depends on hitting right-handers, which would make him a 15-to-18-homer player who scores plenty of runs.","sources":[{"note_id":"n446660284e","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n7784142228","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-second-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Chase Meidroth","team":"CHW","elig":"2B/SS/MI"},"pa7773b6":{"text":"The Yankees took him 39th overall in 2025 and signed him out of high school for $2,797,500, and he went straight to Low-A and hit .353/.457/.441. A hamstring-interrupted 2026 limited him to 32 games, but he reached base 44.8 percent of the time with 39 walks against 31 strikeouts. At 19 in the Arizona Fall League he showed one of the league's most polished left-handed swings, hitting .275/.350/.565 with eight steals in 18 games, and Jim Callis sees a future 20-20 player, though an average arm likely moves him to second base or center field. Razzball's The Itch has his ETA at 2030, so he is a long stash.","sources":[{"note_id":"n43cba80583","article_date":"2026-11-18","source":"Razzball","author":"The Itch","url":"https://razzball.com/new-york-yankees-top-10-prospects-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n2b98ae5da6","article_date":"2026-11-20","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/top-30-arizona-fall-league-prospects-2026"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Dax Kilby","team":"NYY","elig":"SS/MI"},"p5daf106":{"text":"When he plays a full season he produces, and in 2026 he did, hitting .260/.320/.419 with 17 homers, 83 RBIs and 12 steals in 153 games. That followed a lost 2024 and 2025 in which he combined for a .237/.278/.309 line and three homers over 182 games, after a promising 2023 in which he hit .276 with 14 homers in 122 games. He played at least 14 games at each of right, center, left, second, short and third, plus three at first, and that versatility plus the bat give him a path to regular at-bats.","sources":[{"note_id":"ned7ee448c1","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n6c8bd137d1","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-second-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Ezequiel Duran","team":"TEX","elig":"1B/2B/3B/SS/MI/CI/OF"},"pd82e198":{"text":"He stole 39 bases, hit 16 homers and slashed .276/.366/.470 in 93 Triple-A games before a light Rangers debut of .207 with one homer in 58 at-bats. In Texas he played 12 games at second, 11 in center and two at short, and Razzball's Jakkers sees plus speed with a chance for useful power, plus a utility path as Corey Seager ages. ZiPS is cooler and doesn't see him making a 2027 impact.","sources":[{"note_id":"n9bc215c649","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n7618d8aac2","article_date":"2026-11-16","source":"FanGraphs","author":"Dan Szymborski","url":"https://blogs.fangraphs.com/2027-zips-projections-texas-rangers/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Cam Cauley","team":"TEX","elig":"2B/SS/MI/OF"},"p90fbbd1":{"text":"He was one of 2026's late-round gems, hitting 27 homers with 91 RBIs and a .243/.302/.446 line in 145 games, topping 600 plate appearances and 160 combined runs and RBIs. That was a big jump in volume from 2025, when he hit 13 homers with 57 RBIs in 126 games, though his OPS and OPS+ were similar both years. Razzball's Jakkers isn't sure he's a consistent 27-homer catcher, but his minor league .459 slugging and .803 OPS suggest the power is real.","sources":[{"note_id":"n400e7e7ce6","article_date":"2026-10-22","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-catchers-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"n6f3a0dbdee","article_date":"2026-11-06","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Vlad Sedler","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/early-adp-observations-catchers/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Dillon Dingler","team":"DET","elig":"C"},"pbd62888":{"text":"He turns 24 in February and had a solid rookie season for Detroit, hitting .264/.322/.424 with 10 homers, 46 RBIs and a 107 OPS+ in 114 games. He got better as the year went on, from .255 with four homers before the break to .271 with six after it. He also played 41 games at third base, and that versatility helps him find at-bats.","sources":[{"note_id":"n7ea0c890ec","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-second-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nffd6652a16","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"RotoGraphs / FanGraphs","author":"Corbin Young","url":"https://fantasy.fangraphs.com/hitters-who-gained-15-of-value-in-the-second-half-of-2026/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Hao Yu Lee","team":"DET","elig":"2B/3B/MI/CI"},"p77216e9":{"text":"Jim Callis says he has the tools and baseball IQ to be the Yankees' best defensive shortstop since Bucky Dent, and the nine errors he made after his August call-up don't change that view. He reached the majors at 21 and showed his power right away with seven homers and 22 RBIs in 42 games, though he stole only two bases despite real speed. Callis sees the bat speed and quickness for a potential 25-25 shortstop, and Razzball's Jakkers expects the growth to come in stages, with his best years three or four seasons out.","sources":[{"note_id":"ndfce6706e2","article_date":"2026-11-04","source":"MLB.com / MLB Pipeline","author":"Jim Callis","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/ranking-2026-rookies-based-on-long-term-value"},{"note_id":"n564382df65","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"George Lombard Jr.","team":"NYY","elig":"SS/MI"},"p6587b4a":{"text":"Seattle took the 6-foot-5, 220-pound right-hander 55th overall in 2024, and he goes after hitters with a splitter, slider and fastball. As a 20-year-old at Double-A he posted a 3.73 ERA, the same as the year before, and a 1.13 WHIP in 101 1/3 innings with 11.4 strikeouts and just 1.4 walks per nine. Razzball's Grey says he has faced no resistance at any level, though living in the zone can lead to homers, and he wonders aloud whether Sloan is better than Kade Anderson. The path is crowded, with Logan Gilbert, Bryan Woo, George Kirby and Anderson locked into the rotation, so his 2027 innings depend on an opening.","sources":[{"note_id":"n07ec03d47f","article_date":"2026-11-23","source":"Razzball","author":"Grey","url":"https://razzball.com/ryan-sloan-2027-fantasy-outlook/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Ryan Sloan","team":"SEA","elig":"SP"},"p2a96fb1":{"text":"He broke out at 26, hitting .253/.313/.391 with 15 homers, 73 RBIs and 20 steals for Cleveland. He played 143 games at shortstop and started 10 at second, so he carries both spots into 2027. Razzball's Jakkers notes it still added up to only a 99 OPS+, which he sees as about the ceiling: a safe shortstop, not a spectacular one.","sources":[{"note_id":"nd573acbe31","article_date":"2026-11-12","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-shortstops-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"},{"note_id":"nf7897c3d2d","article_date":"2026-11-05","source":"Razzball","author":"Jakkers","url":"https://razzball.com/top-50-dynasty-second-basemen-for-2027-fantasy-baseball/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Brayan Rocchio","team":"CLE","elig":"2B/SS/MI"},"p3451d01":{"text":"Minnesota got him from Tampa Bay in the July 2021 Nelson Cruz trade, three years after the Rays drafted him in the seventh round out of Cal State Stanislaus. He went 7-10 with a 3.79 ERA, 159 strikeouts and just 31 walks over 27 starts and 144 2/3 innings in 2026, but the year was choppy: lower back discomfort in spring, elbow pain that knocked him out after one-third of an inning in May, and a strained left glute in August after three rough post-break starts in which he allowed 16 earned runs in 15 1/3 innings and had trouble repeating his delivery. Six days after the May elbow scare he threw one of his best games of the year, and Derek Shelton called the glute strain nothing serious. The Twins declined their side of his $13 million mutual option for 2027, according to a source, so his salary goes to arbitration, and he remains under club control for one more season before free agency.","sources":[{"note_id":"n84cf1b59fc","article_date":"2021-07-22","source":"MLB.com (Twins press release)","author":"Minnesota Twins","url":"https://www.mlb.com/press-release/press-release-twins-complete-trade-with-tampa-bay-rays"},{"note_id":"n0e6ed8d46e","article_date":"2026-08-07","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/joe-ryan-placed-on-injured-list-with-glute-strain"},{"note_id":"n9b71d7eacb","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"MLB.com","author":"Matthew Leach","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/twins-decline-joe-ryan-mutual-option"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Joe Ryan","team":"MIN","elig":"SP"},"pdf0a965":{"text":"He was the ALCS MVP after allowing one run in 12 innings over three games against Cleveland, capping a turnaround that began when the Rays acquired him from the Mets at the deadline. As a Met he had a 4.99 ERA over 22 starts, but Tampa Bay had him abandon the windup, improve his direction to the plate and lean on his changeup instead of his slider, and he posted a 3.25 ERA in 10 starts, including a 0.96 ERA in September that made him AL Pitcher of the Month. The skeptics point to strikeout rates of 22.1 percent with the Mets and 19.7 percent with the Rays, well below his norm, and a BABIP that fell from .313 to .232, though he is one of two pitchers, with Kevin Gausman, to make at least 30 starts in each of the last four seasons. Baltimore signed him this week to a five-year, $125 million deal with a $10 million signing bonus, the largest for a pitcher in Orioles history, and he joins Shane Baz and Bradish at the front of the rotation.","sources":[{"note_id":"n99c90dd40e","article_date":"2026-11-24","source":"MLB.com","author":"Jake Rill","url":"https://www.mlb.com/news/freddy-peralta-orioles-contract"},{"note_id":"n4fce5eb200","article_date":"2026-11-24","source":"MLB.com (Orioles press release)","author":"Baltimore Orioles","url":"https://www.mlb.com/press-release/press-release-orioles-agree-to-terms-with-right-handed-pitcher-freddy-peralta"},{"note_id":"nc2cf275d9c","article_date":"2026-11-24","source":"The Athletic","author":"Keith Law","url":"https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7809670/2026/11/24/mlb-free-agent-analysis-teruaki-sato-trevor-rogers-freddy-peralta/"},{"note_id":"n233419ab95","article_date":"2026-11-24","source":"MLB Trade Rumors","author":"Anthony Franco","url":"https://www.mlbtraderumors.com/2026/11/orioles-sign-freddy-peralta.html"},{"note_id":"n1505e6599a","article_date":"2026-11-02","source":"FanGraphs","author":"Ben Clemens","url":"https://blogs.fangraphs.com/2027-top-50-free-agents/"}],"as_of":"2026-11-24","approved_by":null,"approved_date":null,"name":"Freddy Peralta","team":"BAL","elig":"SP"}};
